    "objectiveId": "EX-S1-09",
    "objectiveName": "Amazon S3: aws:SourceIp pitfall for requests arriving through VPC endpoints; use aws:SourceVpce instead",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "Secure Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "Secure Architectures",
    "service": "Amazon S3",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A logistics company restricts its reporting bucket with a policy that denies all S3 actions unless aws:SourceIp matches the corporate range 203.0.113.0/24. A new ingestion service on Amazon EC2 instances in private subnets reaches Amazon S3 through a gateway VPC endpoint and receives AccessDenied on every request, while office users are unaffected. The network team confirms the endpoint route table entries are correct, and the security team insists the bucket must remain closed to the public internet. How should a solutions architect restore the service's access?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Add the VPC's private CIDR block to the aws:SourceIp allow list in the bucket policy alongside the corporate range.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "The aws:SourceIp key is evaluated against public IP addresses and does not match requests arriving through a VPC endpoint, so appending the private CIDR leaves the denial in place."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Add a condition to the bucket policy that also permits requests whose aws:SourceVpce value matches the gateway endpoint's ID.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Requests through a gateway endpoint do not carry a public source address the existing condition can match, so allowing the endpoint's ID with aws:SourceVpce admits the service while keeping the bucket closed to the internet."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Route the instances' S3 traffic through a NAT gateway so requests present a public source address that the bucket policy can evaluate.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "A NAT gateway's Elastic IP belongs to AWS address space rather than 203.0.113.0/24, so the condition still fails, and sending S3 traffic over the internet path contradicts the security team's constraint."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Replace the gateway endpoint with an interface endpoint so requests retain their original source address for the bucket policy to evaluate.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "An interface endpoint also delivers requests from private addresses inside the VPC, so the public-range aws:SourceIp condition would continue to reject the service's calls after the swap."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "b"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "b"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "b"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Allow the endpoint with an aws:SourceVpce condition because aws:SourceIp cannot match requests arriving through a VPC endpoint.",
    "explanation": "aws:SourceIp works only for requests that carry a public source address, so traffic through a gateway VPC endpoint can never satisfy a public-CIDR condition. The correct pattern is to add aws:SourceVpce (or aws:SourceVpc) to the policy for in-VPC callers while keeping aws:SourceIp for office traffic. NAT gateways present AWS-owned addresses and push traffic onto the internet path, and interface endpoints share the same private-address behavior.",
