    "objectiveId": "EX-S1-12",
    "objectiveName": "AWS IAM: ABAC with principal/resource tags versus RBAC role explosion across many teams",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "Secure Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "Secure Architectures",
    "service": "AWS IAM",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A consultancy runs 60 project teams in a single development account, with engineers moving between projects every few weeks. The IAM administrators maintain more than 300 project-and-environment roles today, and every new project requires authoring policies before the team can start. Leadership wants new projects to become usable without any policy changes, while guaranteeing that engineers can operate only on resources belonging to their currently assigned project. Which approach meets these requirements?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Standardize a set of customer managed policies for each project and use a pipeline to generate the project's roles automatically whenever a new project is registered.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Automating role generation hides the effort but still creates policy objects per project, so the role count keeps growing and new projects still depend on the pipeline producing policies first."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Grant all engineers a single broad development role and schedule weekly CloudTrail log reviews to detect and escalate any activity against resources outside an engineer's currently assigned project.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Detective review after the fact cannot guarantee engineers operate only on their project's resources, so this fails the preventive isolation requirement even though it removes policy authoring."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Attach resource-based policies to each project's resources that list the engineers currently assigned to the project, updated as assignments change.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Many resource types used in development have no resource-based policies, and maintaining principal lists across frequent reassignments recreates the administrative churn the company wants to eliminate."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Tag resources with a project key, tag each engineer's session with their assigned project, and grant actions in a shared policy only where aws:ResourceTag/project equals aws:PrincipalTag/project.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "With attribute-based access control, one policy comparing principal and resource tags covers all projects, so a new project needs only correct tagging and reassignment is a tag change instead of a policy change."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "d"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "d"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "d"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Use ABAC: compare aws:PrincipalTag/project to aws:ResourceTag/project in one shared policy so new projects need only tags.",
    "explanation": "ABAC replaces per-project role explosion with a single policy whose condition matches the principal's project tag to the resource's project tag, making access follow attributes rather than enumerated grants. New projects become usable the moment resources and sessions carry the right tag, meeting the no-policy-change requirement, and the tag comparison enforces isolation preventively. Generated roles, detective controls, and resource-policy lists all fail one of the two stated constraints.",
