    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "An enterprise authenticates its workforce with an on-premises Active Directory behind AD FS, which supports SAML 2.0. Thirty analysts in the DataAnalysts AD group need AWS Management Console access to a single data-lake account, limited to running Amazon Athena queries and reading two S3 buckets. Corporate policy prohibits creating IAM users, and the account is not part of an AWS organization. Group membership in AD must remain the single source of truth for who receives this access. Which solution meets these requirements?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Provision an IAM user for each of the 30 analysts with a policy scoped to Athena and the two buckets, and synchronize the user membership list against the DataAnalysts AD group with a nightly batch job.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "This directly violates the prohibition on IAM users, and a nightly synchronization job makes IAM the effective source of truth for most of each day rather than the AD group."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Create a SAML identity provider in IAM from the AD FS metadata, create a scoped role trusting the provider for sts:AssumeRoleWithSAML, and map the DataAnalysts group to it in AD FS claim rules.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "This is the standard SAML federation pattern for a single account: the IdP asserts group membership through claim rules, and analysts obtain console sessions on the scoped role without any IAM users existing."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Create an Amazon Cognito user pool federated with AD FS and have analysts sign in through the pool's hosted UI to receive console sessions on a scoped role.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Cognito user pools issue tokens for web and mobile applications and cannot grant AWS Management Console sessions, so the analysts' console requirement cannot be met this way."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Configure the scoped role for sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity and have AD FS issue web identity tokens that analysts exchange for console credentials.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity accepts OIDC tokens from web identity providers, while AD FS in this design is a SAML 2.0 IdP, so the token exchange in this flow is the wrong federation mechanism."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "b"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "b"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "b"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Create a SAML IdP in IAM and a scoped role trusted for AssumeRoleWithSAML, mapped to the AD group via AD FS claim rules.",
    "explanation": "SAML federation to a single account uses an IAM SAML identity provider built from the IdP metadata and a role whose trust policy allows sts:AssumeRoleWithSAML from that provider. AD FS claim rules map the DataAnalysts group to the role, keeping AD as the source of truth while the role's permissions policy confines analysts to Athena and the two buckets. IAM users are prohibited, Cognito user pools cannot open console sessions, and AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity handles OIDC rather than SAML.",
