    "id": "EX-S2-03",
    "objectiveId": "EX-S2-03",
    "objectiveName": "AWS Organizations: Region-lock SCP that must exempt global services via NotAction instead of loosening the Region list.",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "Secure Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "Secure Architectures",
    "service": "AWS Organizations",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A European insurance company must keep all customer data and compute in eu-central-1 and eu-west-1 to satisfy regulators. A solutions architect drafts an SCP with a Deny statement on all actions where the aws:RequestedRegion condition key does not match the two approved Regions and attaches it to the Workloads OU. Within an hour, platform engineers report that they can no longer create IAM roles, update Route 53 hosted zone records, or invalidate CloudFront distributions, although all Regional workloads behave normally. How should the solutions architect correct the SCP while preserving the Region restriction?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Rewrite the Deny statement to use NotAction excluding the IAM, CloudFront, Route 53, Organizations, and Support namespaces, keeping the aws:RequestedRegion condition intact.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Global services route through endpoints outside the approved Regions, so the standard pattern is a Deny with NotAction that exempts global service namespaces while the Region condition still blocks everything else."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Add us-east-1 to the list of Regions in the aws:RequestedRegion condition so that the control plane endpoints of the global services hosted there are no longer blocked by the statement.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Allowing us-east-1 wholesale would let engineers launch Regional resources such as EC2 and S3 there, which violates the data residency requirement the SCP exists to enforce."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Attach a second SCP to the Workloads OU that explicitly allows the IAM, CloudFront, and Route 53 actions in all Regions alongside the existing Deny policy.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "An explicit Deny in any applicable SCP always overrides an Allow, so adding an allow policy at the same OU cannot restore access to the globally scoped actions."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Add an aws:PrincipalArn condition to the Deny statement that exempts the platform engineering roles from the Region restriction across all services.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Exempting specific roles would let those principals create Regional resources anywhere, weakening the residency control, and it would not fix global service access for other principals."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "a"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "a"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "a"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Use a Deny with NotAction that exempts global service namespaces (IAM, CloudFront, Route 53, Organizations, Support) while keeping the aws:RequestedRegion condition.",
    "explanation": "Global services such as IAM, CloudFront, and Route 53 are served from endpoints that do not match the approved Regions, so a blanket Region Deny breaks them. The documented pattern is a Deny statement using NotAction to carve out global service namespaces while the aws:RequestedRegion condition continues to block Regional actions elsewhere. Allowing us-east-1 outright or exempting roles would undermine the residency control, and an Allow SCP can never override an explicit Deny.",
    "trigger": "Region-lock SCP that must exempt global services via NotAction instead of loosening the Region list.",
