    "question": "A gaming company keeps all 25 of its accounts directly under the organization root with a single shared set of SCPs. Production accounts must be locked to two Regions with a fixed list of approved services, while sandbox accounts used by developers should be free to experiment with almost any service, subject only to a few baseline denies. Applying the production restrictions to everyone has caused constant exception requests from developers, and the company expects to add about 10 new accounts of each type over the next year. How should a solutions architect restructure the organization to apply the correct guardrails as it grows?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Create Production and Sandbox OUs under the root, move each account into the matching OU, and attach the strict SCPs to the Production OU and the relaxed baseline to the Sandbox OU.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "OU-level attachment means every current and future account inherits the right guardrails simply by being placed in the correct OU, ending per-account exceptions as the organization grows."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Keep all accounts under the root and attach the strict SCPs individually to each production account and the relaxed SCPs individually to each sandbox account as accounts are created.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Per-account attachment works today but scales poorly; every new account needs manual policy attachment, and accounts also risk hitting the limit on SCPs attached per target."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Tag each account as production or sandbox in Organizations and rewrite the SCPs with condition keys that match on the account tag to apply the appropriate restrictions dynamically.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "SCP statements cannot reliably branch on Organizations account tags for this purpose; policy targeting in Organizations is structural, based on where policies are attached in the root and OU hierarchy."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Split the accounts into two separate organizations, one for production and one for sandbox, and apply a different root-level SCP set in each organization to enforce the two postures.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Two organizations doubles governance surfaces and splits consolidated billing, identity, and sharing, sacrificing single-pane management for a problem OUs solve within one organization."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "a"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "a"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "a"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Create Production and Sandbox OUs and attach the strict and relaxed SCP sets at the OU level so accounts inherit the right guardrails automatically.",
    "explanation": "OUs exist precisely to group accounts by governance posture. Attaching the strict SCP set to a Production OU and a lighter baseline to a Sandbox OU gives each population the correct restrictions through inheritance, and new accounts pick up the right guardrails just by being created in or moved to the right OU. Per-account attachment, tag-conditional SCPs, and separate organizations all add scaling or capability problems.",
    "trigger": "OU structure separating production and sandbox accounts with different SCP strictness.",
