    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "Amazon GuardDuty raises high-severity findings showing that access keys from a member account of a software company were exfiltrated and are launching large GPU instance fleets for cryptocurrency mining. The incident response plan requires the security team to stop all attacker activity in the account within minutes, preserve the account's CloudTrail history, IAM configuration, and running resources for forensic analysis, and retain a path to return the account to normal service after remediation. Which action should the security team take FIRST?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Close the compromised account from the management account so that all API activity stops, then reopen it within the post-closure period once the investigation concludes.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Closure halts the attacker but suspends the account and its resources, hampering live forensic analysis, and restoration within the post-closure window is a heavier, riskier path than policy removal."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Remove the compromised account from the organization so its consolidated billing stops absorbing the mining costs while the security team investigates the activity.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Detaching the account removes it from SCP reach entirely, leaving the attacker with an unconstrained standalone account, the opposite of containment, while billing separation does nothing to stop activity."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Delete all IAM users and roles in the compromised account and rotate the root credentials so that the exfiltrated access keys can no longer authenticate to any service.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Mass-deleting principals destroys the IAM state investigators need, breaks legitimate workloads, and can miss attacker persistence, while doing nothing to stop currently running mining instances via policy."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Move the account into a quarantine OU whose SCP denies all actions except those of the incident-response role, leaving resources and logs in place for investigation.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "A quarantine SCP takes effect immediately, blocks the attacker and all other principals while the IR role investigates, preserves every resource and trail intact, and is fully reversible by moving the account back."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "d"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "d"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "d"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Quarantine via SCP: move the account to a locked-down OU denying everything except the incident-response role — immediate, evidence-preserving, reversible.",
    "explanation": "The quarantine SCP is the containment mechanism that satisfies all three constraints at once: it cuts off attacker API access organization-wide within moments, leaves CloudTrail history, IAM configuration, and running resources untouched for forensics, and is undone by simply moving the account out of the quarantine OU after remediation. Closing the account or detaching it from the organization sacrifices either forensic access or SCP control, and deleting principals destroys evidence without reliably containing the attacker.",
    "trigger": "Compromised-account containment tradeoff: quarantine SCP vs close vs detach from org.",
