    "id": "EX-S3-01",
    "objectiveId": "EX-S3-01",
    "objectiveName": "AWS KMS: Let an AWS service use a CMK via a grant instead of widening the key policy",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "Secure Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "Secure Architectures",
    "service": "AWS KMS",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A media analytics company runs an Auto Scaling group of EC2 instances launched from an AMI whose EBS volumes are encrypted with a customer managed KMS key. Scale-out events are failing because the service-linked role for EC2 Auto Scaling is not authorized to use the key. The security team maintains a deliberately minimal key policy that names only two administrator roles, and it has rejected any change that would permanently broaden the key policy document. The solutions architect must restore scale-out launches and retain the ability to revoke the service's access instantly without redeploying any policy. Which solution meets these requirements?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Add the EC2 Auto Scaling service principal to the key policy document with permissions for kms:Decrypt and kms:GenerateDataKeyWithoutPlaintext on the customer managed key.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "This restores launches but permanently widens the key policy document, which the security team has explicitly rejected, and revocation would require another policy deployment."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Re-create the AMI with volumes encrypted under the AWS managed aws/ebs key so that the Auto Scaling service can use the key without additional authorization.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Moving to the AWS managed key abandons the hardened customer managed key, and the company would lose control over the key policy entirely rather than keeping it minimal."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Have an authorized administrator role create a KMS grant giving the service-linked role the decrypt and data-key operations, and retire the grant to revoke access.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Grants delegate use of a KMS key to a principal without modifying the key policy, and retiring or revoking the grant removes access immediately, satisfying both constraints."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Attach an IAM policy to the service-linked role that allows kms:Decrypt and kms:GenerateDataKeyWithoutPlaintext on the key's ARN.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Service-linked role policies cannot be modified, and an IAM policy alone is ineffective here because the minimal key policy does not delegate authorization decisions to IAM in the account."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "c"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "c"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "c"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Create a KMS grant for the Auto Scaling service-linked role and retire it to revoke access.",
    "explanation": "KMS grants are the mechanism for giving an AWS service or principal scoped, revocable use of a key without editing the key policy. A grant created by an authorized administrator lets the service-linked role decrypt and generate data keys for launches, and retiring the grant revokes access instantly. Widening the key policy or switching to the AWS managed key violates the security team's constraints, and service-linked role policies cannot be edited.",
