    "service": "AWS KMS",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A software vendor distributes firmware updates to hundreds of thousands of field devices. The build pipeline must digitally sign every release artifact, and devices must verify signatures while offline, without calling any AWS API. The security policy states that the signing private key must be generated inside a FIPS-validated hardware security module, must never be exportable, and must be usable only through audited API calls from the pipeline role. Which solution meets these requirements?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Create a KMS HMAC key, have the pipeline call GenerateMac on each artifact, and have devices call VerifyMac to check the tag before installing an update.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "HMAC verification requires access to the same symmetric key inside KMS, so offline devices cannot verify at all, and any verifier that could would need audited access to the shared secret."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Create an asymmetric KMS key with SIGN_VERIFY usage, have the pipeline call kms:Sign for each artifact, and embed the public key in device firmware for offline verification.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "The private key is generated and used only inside KMS HSMs through audited Sign calls, while GetPublicKey exposes just the public half, which devices use to verify signatures with no AWS connectivity."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Call kms:GenerateDataKeyPair, store the returned plaintext private key in AWS Secrets Manager, and have the pipeline retrieve it to sign each release artifact locally on the build servers.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "The plaintext private key leaves KMS, is persisted in Secrets Manager, and is handled on build servers, violating the requirement that the key never be exportable from the HSM."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Use AWS Private CA to issue a code-signing certificate and export its private key to the build servers so the pipeline can sign artifacts with standard tooling.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Exporting the certificate's private key to build servers directly contradicts the policy that the signing key remain non-exportable and used only through audited API calls."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "b"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "b"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "b"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Use an asymmetric KMS SIGN_VERIFY key: sign via kms:Sign, verify offline with the exported public key.",
    "explanation": "Asymmetric KMS keys keep the private key inside FIPS-validated HSMs, usable only through IAM-controlled, CloudTrail-audited Sign requests, while the public key can be freely distributed for offline verification. HMAC keys cannot support offline or third-party verification because both operations need the shared secret, and any design that extracts a private key to Secrets Manager or build servers breaks the non-exportability requirement.",
