    "id": "EX-S4-02",
    "objectiveId": "EX-S4-02",
    "objectiveName": "Amazon S3: Identifying which of the four BPA settings actually stopped an attached public bucket policy",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "Secure Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "Secure Architectures",
    "service": "Amazon S3",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "During a data-center migration, an administrator at a retail company temporarily disabled the BlockPublicPolicy setting on an S3 bucket that holds supplier price lists, leaving the other three Block Public Access settings enabled. A developer then attached a bucket policy granting s3:GetObject to Principal \"*\", and the PutBucketPolicy call succeeded. A later forensic review of the server access logs shows that every anonymous GET request during the exposure window was rejected with 403 Access Denied. Which Block Public Access setting prevented the public reads?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "BlockPublicAcls, because it rejected the public grant at the moment the developer attached the policy statement to the supplier price-list bucket during the migration.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "BlockPublicAcls rejects requests that attach public ACLs to buckets or objects; it has no effect on bucket policies, which is how this grant was made."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "RestrictPublicBuckets, because it ignores cross-account and anonymous access to any bucket whose policy is public, even after the policy is attached.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "RestrictPublicBuckets restricts access to a bucket with a public policy to only the bucket owner's account and AWS services, so anonymous GETs were denied even though the public policy was in place."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "BlockPublicPolicy, because it evaluated the policy document and stripped the public statement before saving it.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "BlockPublicPolicy rejects PutBucketPolicy calls that would make a bucket public, but it was disabled during the migration, which is why the call succeeded, and it never rewrites policy documents."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "IgnorePublicAcls, because it caused S3 to disregard the public grant when evaluating each anonymous request.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "IgnorePublicAcls causes S3 to disregard public ACLs at request time; it does not apply to grants made through a bucket policy."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "b"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "b"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "b"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "RestrictPublicBuckets blocked anonymous access at request time even though the public bucket policy was successfully attached.",
    "explanation": "The two ACL-related settings never apply to bucket policies, and BlockPublicPolicy was disabled, which is exactly why the public policy could be attached at all. RestrictPublicBuckets works at request evaluation time: when a bucket's policy is public, it limits access to the bucket owner's account and AWS services, so the anonymous GETs were denied throughout the exposure window.",
    "trigger": "Identifying which of the four BPA settings actually stopped an attached public bucket policy",
