    "id": "EX-S4-06",
    "objectiveId": "EX-S4-06",
    "objectiveName": "Amazon S3: Correcting a team that used browser-only CORS as if it were S3 access control",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "Secure Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "Secure Architectures",
    "service": "Amazon S3",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A startup hosts customer invoice PDFs in an S3 bucket that its single-page application reads directly. To \"lock the bucket down,\" the frontend team configured a CORS rule allowing only the origin https://app.example.com and considered the work done, leaving the objects readable through a bucket policy that grants s3:GetObject to any principal. A penetration test then downloaded every invoice using curl from an outside laptop. The company must ensure invoices can be retrieved only by its authenticated application users. What should a solutions architect do to meet this requirement?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Tighten the CORS rule to allow only the GET and HEAD methods from the approved origin and set MaxAgeSeconds to zero.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "CORS is enforced by browsers when rendering cross-origin responses; curl and other non-browser clients ignore it entirely, so tightening the rule changes nothing for the attacker."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Add a bucket policy condition that allows s3:GetObject only when the aws:Referer header matches https://app.example.com.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "The Referer header is set by the client and is trivially spoofed with a curl flag, so this still leaves the invoices open to anyone who adds the header."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Remove the public read statement, enable Block Public Access, and have the application backend issue short-lived presigned URLs to authenticated users.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "This makes S3 authorization the control: objects are private, public grants are blocked, and only users the backend has authenticated receive time-limited signed access."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Place the bucket behind a CloudFront distribution and configure the viewer protocol policy to redirect all HTTP requests to HTTPS before they reach the invoice objects.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "A viewer protocol policy only forces encrypted transport; the origin objects remain publicly readable, so any client can still fetch invoices directly or through the distribution."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "c"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "c"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "c"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Make the objects private with BPA and serve authenticated users through backend-issued presigned URLs; CORS is not an access control.",
    "explanation": "CORS only tells browsers which cross-origin pages may read responses; it performs no authentication or authorization, which is why curl bypassed it. The fix is to remove the public grant, turn on Block Public Access, and let the backend hand authenticated users short-lived presigned URLs. Referer conditions are spoofable and HTTPS enforcement does not restrict who can read the objects.",
