    "id": "EX-S4-08",
    "objectiveId": "EX-S4-08",
    "objectiveName": "Amazon S3: Enforcing HTTPS plus a minimum TLS version with paired Deny statements in a bucket policy",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "Secure Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "Secure Architectures",
    "service": "Amazon S3",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A payments company must certify to its acquiring bank that an S3 bucket holding settlement files can never be accessed over unencrypted connections and that encrypted connections always negotiate TLS 1.2 or later. Clients include on-premises batch jobs, EC2 instances in three VPCs, and a partner's SFTP gateway on the internet, so the control must hold no matter where a request originates or which identity makes it. Which bucket policy design meets these requirements?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Add a single Deny statement in the bucket policy for all principals and actions with the condition Bool aws:SecureTransport equals false, applied to the bucket and all of its objects.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "This blocks plaintext HTTP but says nothing about protocol version, so a client could still connect with TLS 1.0 or 1.1 and pass the policy."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Add an Allow statement for the required principals with the condition NumericGreaterThanEquals s3:TlsVersion 1.2.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "A conditional Allow does not forbid anything; requests arriving over weaker TLS or plain HTTP can still succeed through any other statement or identity policy that grants access."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Attach Deny conditions for aws:SecureTransport and s3:TlsVersion to the endpoint policies of the S3 gateway endpoints in the three VPCs.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Endpoint policies only govern traffic that traverses those VPC endpoints, so the on-premises jobs and the internet-facing partner gateway would be entirely outside the control."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Add two Deny statements for all principals and actions: one with condition Bool aws:SecureTransport false, and one with condition NumericLessThan s3:TlsVersion 1.2.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "The paired denies reject plaintext connections and any TLS negotiation below 1.2 for every principal on every path, because an explicit deny in the bucket policy cannot be overridden by any allow."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "d"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "d"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "d"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Pair a Deny on aws:SecureTransport false with a Deny on s3:TlsVersion below 1.2 in the bucket policy.",
    "explanation": "Meeting both halves of the certification takes two explicit Deny statements in the bucket policy: aws:SecureTransport false catches unencrypted requests, and NumericLessThan s3:TlsVersion 1.2 catches legacy TLS negotiations. Because explicit denies override every allow and the bucket policy is evaluated for all origins and identities, the control holds for on-premises, VPC, and internet clients alike.",
