    "id": "EX-S4-09",
    "objectiveId": "EX-S4-09",
    "objectiveName": "Amazon S3: Cross-account replication with re-encryption under the destination account's KMS key",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "Secure Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "Secure Architectures",
    "service": "Amazon S3",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "An insurance company replicates claim documents from a production account's S3 bucket to an archive bucket in a separate security account in another Region. The source objects are encrypted with a customer managed KMS key in the production account. The security account's policy requires that every replica at rest be encrypted under a customer managed KMS key that the security account owns, so that the production account cannot decrypt archived copies after its own key is retired. Replication is currently failing for all KMS-encrypted objects. What should a solutions architect do to meet these requirements?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Update the production key's key policy to grant the security account's reporting and audit roles kms:Decrypt, and keep the replicas encrypted under the production account's key in the destination archive bucket.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "This leaves every replica dependent on a key the production account owns and can retire, directly violating the requirement that archives be protected by the security account's own key."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Convert the production key to a multi-Region key and create a replica key in the archive Region for the destination bucket to use.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Multi-Region replica keys share key material with, and remain owned by, the production account, so the archive would still not be encrypted under a key the security account controls."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Set the destination bucket's default encryption to the security account's KMS key and leave the existing replication rule unchanged.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Replication of SSE-KMS objects requires the rule itself to opt in and name a destination key; default encryption alone does not fix the failing rule or re-encrypt the replicas."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Enable KMS-encrypted object replication in the rule, set the security account's key as the replica key, and grant the replication role decrypt on the source key and encrypt on the destination key.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "The rule's SSE-KMS criteria plus a destination ReplicaKmsKeyID makes S3 re-encrypt each replica under the security account's key, and the role's kms:Decrypt/kms:Encrypt grants let the transfer succeed."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "d"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "d"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "d"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Opt the replication rule into SSE-KMS objects, set the destination account's key as the replica key, and grant the replication role both KMS permissions.",
    "explanation": "S3 replication skips SSE-KMS objects unless the rule explicitly enables them and names a replica KMS key, and cross-account re-encryption additionally needs the replication role authorized for kms:Decrypt on the source key and kms:Encrypt on the destination key through its key policy. Keeping the production key in the destination, whether directly or as a multi-Region replica, leaves the archive under production-account control, and default encryption does not participate in replication's encryption handling.",
