    "id": "EX-S5-08",
    "objectiveId": "EX-S5-08",
    "objectiveName": "AWS Transit Gateway: Asymmetric return path through a multi-AZ inspection VPC — enable TGW appliance mode, not SNAT.",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "Secure Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "Secure Architectures",
    "service": "AWS Transit Gateway",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A company routes all east-west traffic between 14 spoke VPCs through an inspection VPC attached to an AWS Transit Gateway. The inspection VPC runs stateful firewall appliances in two Availability Zones behind Gateway Load Balancer endpoints, and spoke route tables direct inter-VPC traffic to the inspection VPC attachment. Flows between instances that happen to sit in different Availability Zones fail intermittently: the firewalls log the forward packets in one AZ but never see the return packets, which arrive at the appliance in the other AZ and are dropped as unknown flows. The security team requires that original source IP addresses remain visible in firewall logs. What should a solutions architect do to resolve the drops?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Configure the firewall appliances to source-NAT all inspected traffic so return packets are addressed back to the appliance that processed the original flow.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Source NAT would force return traffic through the same appliance, but it rewrites the client address and therefore destroys the original source IP visibility the security team requires in firewall logs."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Enable appliance mode support on the inspection VPC's Transit Gateway attachment so both directions of a flow are sent to the same Availability Zone.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Appliance mode makes the Transit Gateway hash each flow's forward and return traffic to a single Availability Zone in the attachment VPC for the flow's lifetime, so the same stateful appliance sees both directions without any address rewriting."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Disable the source/destination check on the firewall appliances' elastic network interfaces so they forward packets that are not addressed to them.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Source/destination checks govern whether an ENI will forward transit packets at all; they have no influence on which Availability Zone the Transit Gateway selects for return traffic, so the asymmetry remains."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Create separate Transit Gateway route tables per Availability Zone so each spoke's traffic is constrained to the inspection appliances in its own Availability Zone.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Transit Gateway route tables select destinations by CIDR, not by the Availability Zone of the sending instance, so per-AZ route tables cannot pin cross-AZ flows to a single appliance and would add routing complexity without fixing the hashing behavior."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "b"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "b"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "b"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Enable appliance mode on the inspection VPC's TGW attachment to keep both directions of each flow in one AZ.",
    "explanation": "By default a Transit Gateway keeps traffic in the Availability Zone it entered, so a flow's forward and return legs can land on different appliances when endpoints sit in different AZs — exactly the asymmetric drop pattern the stateful firewalls report. Appliance mode changes the attachment's behavior to hash each flow to one AZ for its lifetime, restoring symmetry without NAT, which preserves the required source IP visibility.",
    "trigger": "Asymmetric return path through a multi-AZ inspection VPC — enable TGW appliance mode, not SNAT.",
