    "id": "EX-S5-13",
    "objectiveId": "EX-S5-13",
    "objectiveName": "AWS Shield Advanced: Map L3/4 volumetric and L7 request floods to CloudFront + Shield Advanced + WAF rate-based rules.",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "Secure Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "Secure Architectures",
    "service": "AWS Shield Advanced",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "multiple",
    "question": "A news publisher serves a breaking-news website from EC2 instances behind an internet-facing Application Load Balancer in one Region. During two recent incidents the site was disrupted first by a SYN and UDP reflection flood measured in hundreds of gigabits per second, and a week later by a botnet issuing millions of well-formed HTTP GET requests per minute to expensive article-search URLs. The company wants attacks absorbed as far from the origin as possible, mitigation of both network-layer and request-layer floods, and financial protection against attack-driven usage spikes. Which combination of actions should a solutions architect take? (Choose three.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Place an Amazon CloudFront distribution in front of the Application Load Balancer and restrict the ALB so it accepts traffic only from CloudFront.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "CloudFront absorbs and disperses attack traffic across the global edge, caches cacheable article content, and locking the ALB to CloudFront prevents attackers from bypassing the edge layer to hit the origin directly."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Maintain network ACL deny entries for the source IP ranges observed in previous attacks, updated by a Lambda function that parses VPC Flow Logs.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Reflection floods and botnets rotate and spoof sources faster than reactive deny lists can follow, NACL rule quotas are small, and a hundreds-of-gigabits volumetric flood must be stopped before the VPC, not at the subnet edge."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Subscribe to AWS Shield Advanced and add both the CloudFront distribution and the internet-facing Application Load Balancer as protected resources.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Shield Advanced provides enhanced network-layer DDoS mitigation for the protected resources plus the requested financial safeguards through DDoS cost protection for attack-driven usage spikes."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Deploy intrusion prevention appliances behind a Gateway Load Balancer in the ingress path so attack packets are dropped before reaching the web tier.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "An in-VPC appliance fleet inspects traffic that has already reached the Region and would itself be saturated by a volumetric flood, and IPS signatures do not address well-formed HTTP request floods; it answers an intrusion problem, not a DDoS one."
      },
      {
        "id": "e",
        "text": "Enable Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall with managed domain lists on the VPC so requests from known botnet infrastructure are refused.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "DNS Firewall filters outbound DNS queries made from inside the VPC; it has no effect on inbound HTTP floods or network-layer attacks arriving from the internet."
      },
      {
        "id": "f",
        "text": "Attach an AWS WAF web ACL to the CloudFront distribution with a rate-based rule that limits request rates to the article-search URLs.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "A rate-based rule at the edge throttles the layer 7 GET flood against the expensive search paths — traffic that is well-formed and therefore invisible to network-layer defenses — before it consumes origin capacity."
      }
    ],
