    "id": "EX-S5-14",
    "objectiveId": "EX-S5-14",
    "objectiveName": "Egress-Only Internet Gateway: Outbound-only IPv6 from private subnets — egress-only IGW for ::/0, NAT gateway stays for IPv4.",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "Secure Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "Secure Architectures",
    "service": "Egress-Only Internet Gateway",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A SaaS provider has migrated a dual-stack VPC's private subnets to IPv6 for a batch-processing tier that must download partner datasets from IPv6-only endpoints on the internet. IPv4 egress already works through a NAT gateway in a public subnet. When engineers add IPv6 addresses to the instances, outbound IPv6 connections fail, and the security policy states that internet hosts must never be able to initiate connections to the batch instances over either protocol. Which change enables the required IPv6 connectivity?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Add a ::/0 route in the private subnets' route tables that targets the existing NAT gateway so IPv6 traffic follows the same path as IPv4.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "A NAT gateway performs address translation for IPv4 and cannot serve as the egress path for native IPv6 traffic, so a ::/0 route to it does not deliver the required connectivity."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Add a ::/0 route in the private subnets' route tables that targets the VPC's internet gateway, and rely on security group rules to reject inbound connections.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Routing through the internet gateway makes the globally unique IPv6 addresses reachable from the internet, leaving only security group configuration between the instances and inbound initiation — a posture the never-reachable policy is written to avoid."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Create an egress-only internet gateway for the VPC and add a ::/0 route in the private subnets' route tables that targets the new gateway.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "An egress-only internet gateway is the IPv6 counterpart of the NAT gateway pattern: it forwards outbound IPv6 connections and their stateful returns while dropping all connections initiated from the internet, enforcing the policy at the gateway itself."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Replace the NAT gateway with an egress-only internet gateway and route both the 0.0.0.0/0 and ::/0 destinations of the private subnets through it.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "An egress-only internet gateway handles IPv6 traffic only, so routing 0.0.0.0/0 through it would break the working IPv4 egress that the NAT gateway currently provides."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "c"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "c"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "c"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Add an egress-only internet gateway and point the private subnets' ::/0 route at it, keeping the NAT gateway for IPv4.",
    "explanation": "IPv6 addresses in a VPC are globally unique and are not translated, so the NAT gateway plays no role in IPv6 egress; the egress-only internet gateway exists specifically to give IPv6 instances outbound-only internet access with stateful return handling. The two gateways coexist: NAT gateway for 0.0.0.0/0 and egress-only internet gateway for ::/0, each covering exactly one address family.",
    "trigger": "Outbound-only IPv6 from private subnets — egress-only IGW for ::/0, NAT gateway stays for IPv4.",
