    "id": "EX-S5-15",
    "objectiveId": "EX-S5-15",
    "objectiveName": "VPC Managed Prefix Lists: One CIDR allowlist behind hundreds of SG rules across accounts — shared customer-managed prefix list.",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "Secure Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "Secure Architectures",
    "service": "VPC Managed Prefix Lists",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A logistics company allows inbound access to internal tools only from 32 approved CIDR ranges covering its offices, VPN concentrators, and partner networks. These ranges are currently copied into more than 400 security group rules across dozens of VPCs in six accounts. When a partner network was renumbered last month, engineers spent three days locating and editing every affected rule and still missed several. The company wants the approved ranges maintained in one place, referenced by every security group, and available to all six accounts with the LEAST ongoing effort. Which solution meets these requirements?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Create a customer-managed prefix list containing the approved CIDR ranges, share it with the other accounts by using AWS RAM, and reference the prefix list ID in the security group rules.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Security group rules that reference a prefix list ID inherit its entries automatically, so a renumbering becomes a single prefix list update, and AWS RAM sharing makes the same list usable from all six accounts."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Build an EventBridge-triggered Lambda function that reads the approved ranges from a Parameter Store entry and rewrites the matching rules in every security group across the six accounts.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "This centralizes the source of truth but keeps 400-plus rules as synchronized copies maintained by custom cross-account code the team must own, debug, and extend to each new VPC — far from the least ongoing effort."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Create Amazon VPC IP Address Manager pools for the approved ranges, share the pools across the accounts, and have security group rules draw their allowed sources from the pools.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "IPAM plans and tracks CIDR allocations for VPCs and subnets; security group rules cannot reference IPAM pools as a traffic source, so the copies in each rule would remain."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Create one central security group containing rules for the approved ranges and reference that security group's ID as the source in the rules of every other VPC and account.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "A security group reference matches the instances associated with that group, not the CIDR entries written inside it, and such references do not extend across unpeered VPCs in other accounts — so this neither imports the ranges nor spans the six accounts."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "a"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "a"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "a"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "A customer-managed prefix list shared through AWS RAM and referenced by ID in the security group rules.",
