    "objectiveId": "EX-S6-15",
    "objectiveName": "Amazon S3: Tamper-evident log storage with Object Lock compliance mode and log file validation",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "Secure Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "Secure Architectures",
    "service": "Amazon S3",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "multiple",
    "question": "A public utility must retain CloudTrail logs for 7 years under a regulatory order. During a recent incident, investigators could not prove that delivered log files had gone unaltered, and the regulator now requires two properties: no principal, including administrators of the workload accounts and of the log-archive account itself, may delete or overwrite log files during the retention period, and any modification of an individual log file must be provable after the fact. The organization trail delivers to a bucket in a dedicated log-archive account. Which combination of steps meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Enable S3 Object Lock on the logging bucket in governance mode with a 7-year retention period, granting s3:BypassGovernanceRetention only to the log-archive account's security administrators.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Governance mode is bypassable by any principal holding s3:BypassGovernanceRetention, so the security administrators could still delete log files, failing the no-principal requirement."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Enable S3 Object Lock on the logging bucket in compliance mode with a 7-year retention period so that no principal, including the account's root user, can delete or overwrite the files.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Compliance mode retention cannot be shortened or bypassed by any identity, including root, for the life of the retention period, which is exactly the immutability the regulator demands."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Enable S3 Versioning with MFA delete on the logging bucket so that permanent deletions require the bucket owner's root credentials and MFA device.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "MFA delete still permits the root user with the MFA device to remove versions, so an administrator-level insider or attacker with those credentials defeats the control."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Encrypt the log files with a customer managed KMS key whose key policy denies decryption to the workload accounts, so that the files cannot be modified in place.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Encryption controls who can read the data; a principal with S3 write access can still overwrite or delete objects, and denying decryption proves nothing about integrity."
      },
      {
        "id": "e",
        "text": "Enable CloudTrail log file validation on the organization trail so that signed digest files let investigators prove whether any delivered log file was modified or removed.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Log file validation produces cryptographically signed hourly digests that chain the delivered files together, giving investigators the after-the-fact tamper evidence the regulator requires."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "b",
      "e"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "b",
      "e"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "b",
      "e"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Use S3 Object Lock in compliance mode for 7-year immutability and CloudTrail log file validation for cryptographic tamper evidence.",
    "explanation": "The regulator's two properties map to two distinct controls. Object Lock in compliance mode makes deletion or overwrite impossible for every principal, including root in the log-archive account, for the full retention period, whereas governance mode and MFA delete both leave privileged escape hatches. Provability of integrity comes from CloudTrail log file validation, whose signed digest chain lets investigators demonstrate whether any file was altered or removed after delivery.",
