    "type": "multiple",
    "question": "A healthcare company runs an internal claims-processing REST API on Amazon API Gateway with a regional endpoint. The API is called only by EC2 instances in a single VPC, but a penetration test showed that the API's public execute-api URL is reachable from the internet by anyone holding valid credentials. Compliance requires that all traffic to the API stay on the AWS network and that any request arriving over a public path be rejected. Which combination of steps meets these requirements? (Choose two.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Create a gateway VPC endpoint for API Gateway in the VPC and add a route table entry that directs execute-api traffic through the endpoint.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Gateway VPC endpoints exist only for S3 and DynamoDB. API Gateway requires an interface endpoint, so this routing configuration cannot be created."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Convert the API to a private endpoint type and create an interface VPC endpoint for execute-api in the VPC with private DNS enabled.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "A private endpoint type removes the API from the public network entirely, and the interface endpoint keeps invocations from the EC2 instances on the AWS network."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Associate a regional AWS WAF web ACL with the API stage and add a rule that blocks any request whose source IP address is outside the VPC CIDR range.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Traffic leaving the VPC over the public path carries a public source address, not the private CIDR, and this leaves the public execute-api hostname in place rather than removing it."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Place the API behind an internal Application Load Balancer and update the EC2 instances to call the load balancer DNS name instead of the execute-api URL.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Changing what the clients call does not disable the public execute-api endpoint, so internet callers with valid credentials could still reach the API directly."
      },
      {
        "id": "e",
        "text": "Attach a resource policy to the API that denies execute-api:Invoke for any request whose aws:sourceVpce value does not match the interface endpoint ID.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "A private API requires a resource policy before it can be deployed, and pinning aws:sourceVpce to the specific endpoint rejects requests arriving over any other path."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "b",
      "e"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "b",
      "e"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "b",
      "e"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Make the API private with an interface VPC endpoint for execute-api, and attach a resource policy that requires the specific aws:sourceVpce value.",
    "explanation": "Both halves are needed together: the private endpoint type plus the interface VPC endpoint keep invocation traffic on the AWS network, and the resource policy pinned to aws:sourceVpce is what actually rejects requests that do not arrive through that endpoint. WAF CIDR rules and client-side URL changes leave the public path intact, and gateway endpoints do not exist for API Gateway.",
    "trigger": "Private REST API via interface VPC endpoint plus aws:sourceVpce resource policy to close the public path",
