    "id": "EX-S7-03",
    "objectiveId": "EX-S7-03",
    "objectiveName": "Amazon API Gateway: Lambda authorizer cache keyed on a non-unique identity source lets users ride another user's cached allow",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "Secure Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "Secure Architectures",
    "service": "Amazon API Gateway",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A B2B SaaS platform exposes a REST API on Amazon API Gateway with a Lambda REQUEST authorizer that validates a bearer token and returns an IAM policy scoped to the individual caller's permissions. To cut latency, the team enabled authorization caching with a 300-second TTL and set the cache identity source to a custom X-Client-Version header that the mobile app sends with every call. Soon afterward, customers reported that some users could briefly perform actions that were permitted only for other users. What should a solutions architect do to fix the issue while keeping caching enabled?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Change the authorizer to the TOKEN type while keeping the X-Client-Version header as its identity source so the cached policy is revalidated against the header on every request.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "The authorizer type does not change how the cache is keyed. As long as the cache key is a header shared by many users, one user's cached policy is still served to others."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Configure the authorizer to return a policy whose Resource is limited to the single method that was invoked so a cached policy cannot be applied to any other method.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Narrowing the resource restricts which methods a cached policy covers, but the cache entry is still shared across every user who sends the same X-Client-Version value."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Change the identity source to the Authorization header that carries the bearer token so the cache stores a separate policy entry for each distinct token that is presented.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "The cache is keyed on the identity source, so keying it on the per-user bearer token gives every user an isolated cache entry and preserves the latency benefit of caching."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Disable authorization caching for the stage and add per-key usage plan throttling to compensate for the additional Lambda authorizer invocations that will result.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Disabling the cache would stop the cross-user policy reuse, but it violates the requirement to keep caching enabled, and throttling does not replace the lost latency benefit."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "c"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "c"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "c"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Key the authorizer cache on the Authorization header so each bearer token gets its own cached policy.",
    "explanation": "API Gateway caches the authorizer's returned policy keyed on the configured identity source. Because X-Client-Version is identical across many users, the first caller's allow policy was served to everyone sharing that header value for up to 300 seconds. Keying the cache on the per-user Authorization header isolates entries per token while keeping the caching the team wanted.",
    "trigger": "Lambda authorizer cache keyed on a non-unique identity source lets users ride another user's cached allow",
