    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A logistics company exposes a REST API on Amazon API Gateway to 40 B2B partners over the internet. A new contract requires every partner to authenticate with an X.509 client certificate issued by the company's private certificate authority, and the company must be able to onboard partners and rotate their certificates without redeploying the API. The security team also requires that partners cannot skip the certificate check by calling the API's default endpoint. Which solution meets these requirements?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Generate an API Gateway client certificate for the stage, distribute copies of it to all 40 partners, and require them to present the certificate on every request to the API's custom domain name.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "The API Gateway client certificate feature authenticates the gateway to backend integrations, not callers to the gateway, and sharing one certificate gives no per-partner identity or rotation path."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Create a custom domain name with mutual TLS enabled, reference a CA truststore bundle in a versioned S3 bucket, publish a new bundle version when certificates change, and disable the default execute-api endpoint.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Mutual TLS on a custom domain validates partner certificates against the S3 truststore, updating the bundle version rotates trust without redeploying, and disabling the default endpoint closes the bypass."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Import each partner's certificate into AWS Certificate Manager, associate the imported certificates with the API's stage settings, and rotate them by re-importing replacement certificates before each one expires so that partner trust never lapses.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "ACM holds server certificates for domain names and cannot be attached to a stage as a set of trusted client certificates, so this configuration cannot enforce partner authentication."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Place an Application Load Balancer with mutual TLS in verify mode in front of the API, upload the CA bundle to its trust store, and forward authenticated partner traffic to the default execute-api endpoint.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "The load balancer would verify certificates, but the default execute-api endpoint it forwards to remains publicly reachable, so partners could still call the API directly and skip the check."
      }
    ],
