    "service": "AWS WAF",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "An online retailer serves its storefront through an Amazon CloudFront distribution whose origin is an internet-facing Application Load Balancer in us-east-1. During a bot attack, the security team attached rate-based rules to a regional web ACL on the load balancer, keyed on the X-Forwarded-For header, but attackers evaded the limits by spoofing that header and by sending requests straight to the load balancer's public DNS name. The team wants rate limits evaluated against genuine client IP addresses and wants traffic that bypasses CloudFront to be rejected. Which solution meets these requirements?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Keep the regional web ACL on the load balancer, enable its forwarded-IP configuration with a fallback behavior of block, and add a second rate-based rule that is keyed on the connection's source IP address.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Attackers reaching the load balancer directly can still forge X-Forwarded-For, and a source-IP rule at the origin counts CloudFront edge addresses for legitimate traffic, so neither requirement is met."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Associate the existing regional web ACL with the CloudFront distribution as well, so the same rate-based rules are evaluated both at the edge locations and again at the regional Application Load Balancer behind it.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Web ACL scopes are not interchangeable; a REGIONAL web ACL cannot be associated with a CloudFront distribution, which requires a web ACL created in the CLOUDFRONT scope."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Enable AWS Shield Advanced on both the distribution and the load balancer so the Shield Response Team applies rate limits and blocks the requests that arrive outside the distribution.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Shield Advanced targets DDoS detection and response and does not provide a mechanism that rejects ordinary requests sent directly to the load balancer's public DNS name."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Create a CloudFront-scope web ACL with the rate-based rules on the distribution, have CloudFront add a secret custom header to origin requests, and add a listener rule that rejects requests missing that header.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "At the edge, WAF sees the true client IP address, so rate limits work as intended, and the secret header check on the load balancer rejects any request that did not pass through the distribution."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "d"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "d"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "d"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Attach a CloudFront-scope web ACL with rate-based rules at the edge, and lock the ALB to CloudFront with a secret origin header verified by a listener rule.",
    "explanation": "Rate limiting must move to the CloudFront-scope web ACL, where AWS WAF evaluates the actual connecting client IP instead of a spoofable X-Forwarded-For header or CloudFront's own edge addresses. Pairing that with a secret custom header injected by CloudFront and enforced by an ALB listener rule closes the direct-to-origin path, so both stated requirements are satisfied together.",
