    "service": "Amazon API Gateway",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A fintech company runs 12 internal microservices on Amazon ECS that call one another through REST APIs hosted on Amazon API Gateway. Every service currently sends the same static API key, which is copied into each task definition and has not been rotated in over a year. A security review requires that each calling service present a distinct, automatically rotated credential and be able to invoke only the specific API resources it depends on. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Enable AWS_IAM authorization on the API methods, grant each service's task role execute-api:Invoke on only the resources it depends on, and have the services sign requests with Signature Version 4.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Task roles give every service a distinct identity with credentials that ECS vends and rotates automatically, and execute-api:Invoke scoped to specific resources enforces per-route least privilege with no secrets to manage."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Store a unique API key for each service in AWS Secrets Manager with automatic rotation enabled, and attach each key to a usage plan that includes only the API stages the service depends on.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "API keys are a metering construct rather than an authentication mechanism, and rotating them requires custom rotation functions plus coordinated updates in API Gateway, adding ongoing overhead."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Create a Cognito user pool app client for each service using the client credentials grant, and configure a Cognito authorizer on the API methods to validate the OAuth access tokens and their scopes.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "This can work for machine-to-machine auth, but it introduces a user pool, per-service app clients, client secrets to protect, and scope management, which is more operational overhead than IAM roles the tasks already have."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Issue each service a certificate from AWS Private CA, enable mutual TLS on a custom domain name for the APIs, and map each certificate's subject name to the API resources the service is allowed to invoke.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Mutual TLS authenticates the connection but provides no native per-resource authorization mapping, so enforcing route-level access would still require custom logic plus certificate lifecycle management."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "a"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "a"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "a"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Use AWS_IAM authorization with SigV4 signing and scope each task role's execute-api:Invoke permission to the routes it needs.",
    "explanation": "ECS task roles already give every microservice a distinct IAM identity whose temporary credentials are vended and rotated automatically, so switching the APIs to AWS_IAM authorization removes shared secrets entirely. Scoping execute-api:Invoke to specific resources and methods delivers the per-route least privilege the review demands, and there is nothing to store, distribute, or rotate manually.",
    "trigger": "Replace shared API keys with IAM SigV4 and resource-scoped execute-api:Invoke for service-to-service calls",
