    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A streaming service delivers a subscriber-only video library through an Amazon CloudFront distribution with an S3 origin that is locked down with origin access control. Each playback session loads an HLS manifest plus several thousand short video segments, and the licensed third-party player cannot modify individual segment URLs after the manifest is loaded. Access must end promptly when a subscription lapses. Which solution meets these requirements?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Generate a CloudFront signed URL for every segment listed in the manifest at the start of each session, setting each URL's expiration to match the end of the subscriber's current billing period.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Signing thousands of individual segment URLs per session does not work here because the player cannot rewrite segment URLs after it loads the manifest."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Issue CloudFront signed cookies with a custom policy covering the video path pattern after each subscriber authenticates, with an expiration aligned to the subscriber's current status.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Signed cookies authorize every request matching the path pattern without altering any URLs, which suits thousands of unmodifiable segment requests, and short expirations cut off lapsed subscribers."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Have the backend create S3 presigned URLs for the manifest and every segment and return them to the player, so authenticated subscribers fetch the files from the bucket directly.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "This has the same per-URL rewriting problem as signed URLs and also bypasses CloudFront, losing edge caching and conflicting with the origin access control lockdown."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Attach an AWS WAF web ACL to the distribution with a rule that allows segment requests only when they carry a session header that the application adds after each subscriber signs in.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "WAF can match a header's presence but cannot cryptographically validate a session, and the third-party player cannot be relied on to attach custom headers to every segment request."
      }
    ],
