    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "Secure Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "Secure Architectures",
    "service": "Amazon API Gateway",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "multiple",
    "question": "A subscription-billing provider delivers event webhooks to a company's endpoint, which is an Amazon API Gateway REST API with a Lambda integration. Each webhook carries an HMAC-SHA256 signature header computed with a shared signing secret and a timestamp header. The provider does not support presenting client certificates and its egress IP addresses change without notice. During an incident, an attacker who had captured old webhook payloads replayed them unmodified, causing duplicate order fulfillment even though the signatures were valid. Which combination of steps should a solutions architect take to secure the endpoint? (Choose two.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "In the Lambda function, recompute the HMAC over the raw body using the signing secret from AWS Secrets Manager and reject any request whose signature header does not match.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Verifying the HMAC against the raw body authenticates that each event genuinely originated from the provider and was not altered, with the shared secret kept out of code and configuration."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Require an API key on the webhook method and attach a usage plan so that the provider must include a valid x-api-key header with every one of its event deliveries.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "API keys identify callers for metering and throttling rather than authenticating them, and a captured request already contains the key, so replays would still be accepted."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Attach a resource policy to the API that allows invocations of the webhook method only from the source IP address ranges that the billing provider currently uses for its webhook delivery infrastructure.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "The provider's egress addresses change without notice, so an IP allowlist would break legitimate deliveries, and it validates the network path rather than the message itself."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Reject events whose timestamp header is older than a short tolerance window, and record processed event IDs in a DynamoDB table with TTL so any event ID already seen is discarded.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "The timestamp window bounds how long a captured payload stays usable, and the deduplication table rejects an already-processed event ID even inside the window, which stops the replay pattern seen in the incident."
      },
      {
        "id": "e",
        "text": "Enable mutual TLS on the API's custom domain name and require the billing provider to present a client certificate signed by the company's private CA with every delivery.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "The provider explicitly does not support client certificates, so this configuration would cause every legitimate webhook delivery to fail the TLS handshake."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "a",
      "d"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "a",
      "d"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "a",
      "d"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Verify the HMAC signature over the raw body with the secret from Secrets Manager, and enforce a timestamp freshness window plus event-ID deduplication.",
