    "service": "Amazon API Gateway",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A financial dashboard is a single-page application served from https://app.example.com that calls a REST API hosted on Amazon API Gateway at https://api.example.com. The browser sends a JWT in the Authorization header and a session cookie with the fetch credentials option set to include, and every call is currently blocked with CORS preflight errors in the console. Corporate security policy prohibits allowing arbitrary or wildcard origins on the API. Which configuration resolves the browser errors while complying with the policy?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Enable CORS on each resource with Access-Control-Allow-Origin set to * so that preflight requests from any subdomain of example.com succeed without the origins being listed individually.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "A wildcard origin directly violates the stated policy, and browsers additionally refuse to expose responses to credentialed requests when the allowed origin is the wildcard."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Add the Access-Control-Allow-Origin header to the 200 responses of each integration and omit the OPTIONS method because GET and POST requests are simple requests that skip preflight.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Requests carrying an Authorization header are never simple requests, so the browser always sends an OPTIONS preflight, which fails when no method exists to answer it."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Configure the OPTIONS method to return Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * together with Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true so that both the Authorization header and the session cookie are accepted by the browser during its preflight check.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Browsers reject the combination of a wildcard origin with credentials, so the credentialed fetch still fails, and the wildcard also breaches the corporate policy."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Configure an OPTIONS mock integration and the 4XX gateway responses to return Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.example.com, list Authorization in Access-Control-Allow-Headers, and set Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Naming the exact origin satisfies the policy and the credentialed-request rules, allowing the Authorization header clears the preflight, and adding the headers to gateway responses keeps errors readable by the app."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "d"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "d"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "d"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Return the exact origin with Allow-Credentials true and Authorization in the allowed headers on both the OPTIONS preflight and the gateway responses.",
    "explanation": "An Authorization header always forces a preflight, and a credentialed request requires the response to name the exact calling origin with Access-Control-Allow-Credentials set to true, because the wildcard is rejected by both the browser and the corporate policy. Configuring the OPTIONS mock integration plus the 4XX gateway responses ensures the CORS headers are present even on errors that never reach the integration, so the SPA can read failures instead of seeing opaque CORS blocks.",
