    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "Secure Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "Secure Architectures",
    "service": "Amazon EC2",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A media company runs containerized workloads on Amazon ECS with the EC2 launch type. The tasks use bridge network mode and call the instance metadata service at startup to retrieve the Region and instance identity. A recent penetration test showed that a compromised task could use IMDSv1 to steal instance role credentials, so the security team mandated session-based metadata requests on every container host. After an engineer set HttpTokens to required and HttpPutResponseHopLimit to 1 on the instances, the tasks began failing at startup. What should a solutions architect do to satisfy the security mandate while restoring task functionality?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Revert HttpTokens to optional on the container instances and block access to 169.254.169.254 from the tasks with an outbound security group rule.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Reverting to optional re-enables IMDSv1 and violates the mandate, and security groups do not filter traffic to the link-local instance metadata address."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Keep HttpTokens set to required on the container instances and raise HttpPutResponseHopLimit from 1 to 2 so token responses can reach the tasks.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "A hop limit of 2 lets the IMDSv2 PUT token response cross the extra network hop that bridge-mode containers introduce, while IMDSv1 stays disabled on the hosts."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Disable the instance metadata service on the container hosts entirely and inject the Region and identity values into every task as environment variables.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Disabling IMDS breaks the ECS agent and instance role credential delivery on the hosts, and hardcoding identity values into task definitions adds ongoing maintenance."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Keep HttpPutResponseHopLimit at 1 on the instances and move the tasks to awsvpc network mode so each task queries the metadata service directly.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "In awsvpc mode a task's ENI cannot reach the EC2 instance metadata service at all, so the startup calls would still fail and the change forces a redeployment of every service."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "b"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "b"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "b"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Require IMDSv2 (HttpTokens=required) and set HttpPutResponseHopLimit to 2 so bridge-mode containers can obtain session tokens.",
    "explanation": "IMDSv2 token responses are IP packets whose TTL is set by HttpPutResponseHopLimit. Bridge-mode containers sit one network hop away from the host, so a hop limit of 1 causes the token response to be dropped before it reaches the task. Raising the limit to 2 restores container access while keeping IMDSv1 disabled and the host protected.",
    "trigger": "IMDSv2 hop limit 2 for containers on EC2 container hosts",
    "intentGroup": "sec-data-imdsv2-hop-limit",
