    "id": "EX-S8-03",
    "objectiveId": "EX-S8-03",
    "objectiveName": "Amazon RDS: Which RDS change truly closes public exposure: the PubliclyAccessible flag, not SG/NACL/subnet-group edits",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "Secure Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "Secure Architectures",
    "service": "Amazon RDS",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A Security Hub finding reports that an Amazon RDS for MySQL instance in a production VPC is publicly accessible. The DB instance was launched into a DB subnet group containing public subnets, its security group allows inbound TCP 3306 from 0.0.0.0/0, and the PubliclyAccessible attribute is set to true. Company policy states that the database endpoint must not resolve to a public IP address, and the internal applications in the VPC must keep connecting without interruption. Which change should a solutions architect make FIRST to eliminate the public exposure?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Modify the DB instance to set the PubliclyAccessible attribute to false so its endpoint resolves to the private IP address.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Disabling public accessibility removes the public IP mapping so the endpoint resolves privately from everywhere, directly satisfying the policy while VPC clients keep connecting."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Replace the rule in the security group so that inbound TCP 3306 is allowed only from the CIDR ranges of the application subnets.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Tightening the security group filters traffic but the endpoint still resolves to a public IP address, so the stated policy is still violated and the finding persists."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Modify the DB instance to use a new DB subnet group that contains only private subnets located in the same production VPC.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "RDS does not allow changing a DB instance to a different DB subnet group within the same VPC, so this remediation cannot be applied to the running instance."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Associate a network ACL with the public subnets that denies inbound TCP 3306 from any source outside the VPC CIDR range.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "A network ACL blocks packets at the subnet edge but does not remove the public IP mapping from the endpoint, and a broad subnet-level deny risks affecting other resources."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "a"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "a"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "a"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Set PubliclyAccessible to false on the DB instance; that removes the public IP mapping while internal clients continue resolving the private address.",
    "explanation": "The PubliclyAccessible attribute is what causes the RDS endpoint to map to a public IP address. Setting it to false makes the endpoint resolve only to the private IP, which is the change that actually satisfies the no-public-resolution policy. Security group or NACL tightening only filters traffic, and a DB subnet group cannot be swapped within the same VPC.",
    "trigger": "Which RDS change truly closes public exposure: the PubliclyAccessible flag, not SG/NACL/subnet-group edits",
