    "objectiveName": "Amazon Route 53: Prevent false Regional failovers by raising health check thresholds and aggregating endpoints with calculated health checks.",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "Resilient Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "Resilient Architectures",
    "service": "Amazon Route 53",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "multiple",
    "question": "A travel-booking platform uses Amazon Route 53 failover routing between its primary Region and a warm standby Region. The failover record is tied to a single health check on one of the three public web endpoints, using a 10-second request interval and a failure threshold of 1. Last month a 30-second network blip on that endpoint triggered a full Regional failover even though the other two endpoints were healthy, causing an unnecessary cutover. The team wants failover to occur only during genuine Regional impairment. Which combination of changes should a solutions architect make? (Choose two.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Keep the fast 10-second request interval on the health check so that endpoint status is refreshed as rapidly as possible after transient failure results are observed.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Retaining the fast interval with a low threshold preserves the hair-trigger sensitivity that caused the false failover, doing nothing to filter out brief blips."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Associate the failover records with a CloudWatch alarm that marks the Region unhealthy as soon as a single web request to any endpoint fails.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Alarming on a single failed request is even more sensitive than the current configuration and would convert ordinary transient errors into Regional failovers."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Create a calculated health check with child health checks on all three web endpoints, reporting unhealthy only when healthy children fall below a set threshold.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "A calculated health check aggregates multiple endpoints, so one flapping endpoint no longer speaks for the whole Region and failover requires broad, genuine impairment."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Raise the failure threshold and use the standard 30-second request interval so an endpoint must fail several consecutive checks before it is considered unhealthy.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "A higher failure threshold with the standard interval requires a sustained outage lasting minutes rather than seconds before Route 53 declares the endpoint unhealthy, filtering out momentary blips."
      },
      {
        "id": "e",
        "text": "Lower the TTL on the failover DNS records so client resolvers return to the primary Region's endpoints more quickly after any unnecessary failover completes.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "A lower TTL shortens recovery after a false failover has already happened, but it does not reduce the likelihood of the false failover occurring in the first place."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "c",
      "d"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "c",
      "d"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "c",
      "d"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "c and d — Aggregate the three endpoints with a calculated health check and raise the failure threshold with a standard interval.",
    "explanation": "False failovers stem from a single endpoint's momentary state deciding the fate of an entire Region. A calculated health check widens the evidence base to all three endpoints, while a higher failure threshold with the standard 30-second interval demands sustained failure before an endpoint is marked unhealthy. Faster intervals, single-request alarms, and TTL tuning either amplify sensitivity or only ease recovery after the mistake.",
