    "service": "Amazon SQS",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A ticketing platform publishes seat-reservation commands to an Amazon SQS FIFO queue. Every message currently uses the single message group ID \"reservations\", and producers send messages individually without batching. During popular on-sales, producers are throttled at the queue's limit of 300 messages per second, and the platform forecasts peaks of 9,000 messages per second. Reservations for the same event must be processed in the order they were submitted, but different events are completely independent of one another. Which change lets the platform reach the forecast throughput while preserving the required ordering?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Replace the FIFO queue with a standard queue and include a sequence number in each message payload so consumers can reorder reservations for an event.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "A standard queue removes the throughput ceiling but provides only best-effort ordering, forcing every consumer to buffer and re-sequence messages, which reintroduces ordering risk the FIFO queue was chosen to avoid."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Create thirty separate FIFO queues and modify producers to shard reservation traffic across the queues by hashing each reservation's event identifier.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Sharding across many queues can reach the throughput number but forces the team to manage queue fleets, consumer assignments, and rebalancing that high-throughput mode on a single queue makes unnecessary."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Enable high throughput mode on the FIFO queue and set each message's group ID to its own event identifier so traffic spreads across many message groups.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "High throughput FIFO raises the queue-level ceiling dramatically, and because throughput and ordering are scoped per message group, keying groups by event ID scales in parallel while preserving order within each event."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Keep the single message group ID and have producers send messages in batches of ten so the queue accepts up to ten times as many reservations per second.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Batching raises the per-queue send limit to about 3,000 messages per second, which is still below the forecast, and a single message group also serializes consumption so processing cannot scale out."
      }
    ],
