    "service": "Amazon EBS",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A fintech company uses Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager to take nightly snapshots of a 2 TB gp3 volume that backs its ledger database, retaining 30 days of snapshots. During a compliance review, an auditor objects to the automated deletion of the oldest snapshot each night, arguing that because EBS snapshots are incremental, removing older snapshots will leave newer snapshots unable to restore blocks they depend on. The company wants to keep the 30-day retention policy and its storage costs under control. How should the solutions architect respond?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Retain the first snapshot of the volume indefinitely because it is the only full copy, and allow the policy to delete only the incremental snapshots taken after it.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "There is no special full snapshot that later snapshots depend on for restores. EBS manages block references automatically, so no snapshot must be pinned to keep the chain valid."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Copy each snapshot to a second Region before it is deleted so that any blocks referenced by the remaining snapshots stay available for future restore operations.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Cross-Region copies add cost and are unnecessary for chain integrity. Blocks needed by retained snapshots are preserved in the source Region without any copies."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Approve the deletions because a deleted snapshot's shared blocks are preserved for the remaining snapshots, so every retained snapshot can still restore the full volume.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Although snapshots are stored incrementally, each one is self-sufficient for restores. Deleting a snapshot removes only the data unique to it, and shared blocks are kept for the surviving snapshots."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Convert the lifecycle policy to create EBS-backed AMIs instead, because AMIs consolidate the incremental snapshots into full standalone images that can each be deleted independently.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "AMIs are built on the same incremental snapshot mechanism and do not consolidate anything. Switching to AMI policies adds artifacts to manage without changing snapshot semantics."
      }
    ],
