    "type": "single",
    "question": "An e-commerce company stores its product catalog in a versioned S3 bucket that batch jobs overwrite daily. The operations runbook requires that any overwritten object be recoverable to its prior state for 60 days, which is how the team has resolved several bad-deployment incidents. A cost review shows the bucket has grown from 8 TB to 41 TB in a year, almost entirely from accumulated noncurrent versions that are never cleaned up. Which solution meets the recovery requirement MOST cost-effectively?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Create a lifecycle rule that expires current object versions after 60 days and removes any expired object delete markers left behind in the bucket.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Expiring current versions targets the live catalog data rather than the accumulated history. This would start deleting objects the application still serves while leaving old noncurrent versions in place."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Create a lifecycle rule that transitions noncurrent versions to S3 Intelligent-Tiering after 30 days so infrequently accessed history moves to cheaper access tiers.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Tiering lowers the per-GB price but never deletes anything, so the version count and total footprint keep growing without bound. The 60-day requirement implies old versions can be removed entirely."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Suspend versioning on the bucket and use a daily S3 Batch Operations job to copy each day's objects into a dated prefix that is retained for 60 days.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "This rebuilds version history by duplicating the full catalog every day, which multiplies storage instead of reducing it. It also adds a custom job to operate where a native lifecycle action suffices."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Create a lifecycle rule with a NoncurrentVersionExpiration action that permanently deletes noncurrent versions 60 days after they become noncurrent.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Noncurrent version expiration keeps every overwritten object recoverable for exactly the required 60-day window and then deletes it. This bounds storage growth with no custom tooling and no impact on current versions."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "d"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "d"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "d"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "d — expire noncurrent versions 60 days after they become noncurrent.",
    "explanation": "The two constraints are a fixed 60-day restore window and bounded storage growth, and NoncurrentVersionExpiration satisfies both natively: versions stay restorable for the window and are then permanently removed. Expiring current versions attacks live data, tiering reduces price but not growth, and suspending versioning with daily copies duplicates the dataset while abandoning the mechanism that made restores easy.",
