    "id": "EX-R5-09",
    "objectiveId": "EX-R5-09",
    "objectiveName": "Amazon EBS: RAID-striped EBS volumes need one atomic multi-volume snapshot set, not four separate snapshots.",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "Resilient Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "Resilient Architectures",
    "service": "Amazon EBS",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A gaming company self-manages PostgreSQL on an EC2 instance with the data directory striped in RAID 0 across four gp3 volumes for throughput. The service runs 24/7 and the company cannot stop the instance or unmount the array for backups. A recent restore test failed because the four volume snapshots were taken minutes apart, leaving the reassembled stripe set inconsistent and the database unable to recover. The company needs nightly backups from which the database can reliably perform crash recovery. What should a solutions architect recommend?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Configure four Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager policies, one per volume, that all trigger at the same nightly time so the snapshots begin together.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Separate policies create independent snapshots with no shared point-in-time boundary, even when scheduled identically. The stripes can still capture different moments, reproducing the failed restore."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Stop the database instance for a short nightly window and snapshot each of the four volumes before starting the instance again.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Stopping the instance would indeed produce consistent snapshots, but the workload is explicitly 24/7 and cannot take a nightly outage. The requirement must be met without interrupting the service."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Run a nightly script that snapshots the four volumes back-to-back in rapid sequence during the lowest-traffic hour to minimize the interval between the captures.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Minimizing the gap between snapshots does not eliminate it, and any writes landing between captures still skew the stripe set. Reduced probability of corruption is not the reliable recovery the company requires."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Use the EBS multi-volume snapshot capability against the instance so all four attached volumes are captured as one crash-consistent snapshot set nightly.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Multi-volume snapshots pause I/O across all of the instance's attached volumes and capture them at a single point in time. The restored stripe set then looks like a sudden power loss, which PostgreSQL crash recovery handles by design."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "d"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "d"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "d"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "d — take crash-consistent multi-volume snapshot sets across all four volumes in one operation.",
    "explanation": "A striped array is only restorable if every member reflects the same instant, and the EBS multi-volume snapshot capability (via the CreateSnapshots API or a DLM instance-targeted policy) captures all attached volumes as one crash-consistent set. The database then recovers exactly as it would after a crash. Aligned schedules and rapid sequential scripts still allow inter-snapshot writes, and stopping the instance violates the 24/7 constraint.",
