    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A logistics company runs its dispatch database on an Aurora Global Database with the primary cluster in us-east-1 and a secondary cluster in us-west-2. A widespread service disruption makes the primary Region's database and its APIs unreachable, and the incident commander orders recovery in us-west-2 within 15 minutes. After service is restored, the business stakeholders must be told exactly how much committed data was lost. Which course of action meets the recovery objective and correctly determines the data loss?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Perform a managed planned switchover of the global database to us-west-2, then report zero data loss because the switchover process synchronizes the clusters before changing roles.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "A managed switchover requires a healthy, reachable primary cluster so replication can drain first; it cannot be used while the primary Region is down, making it the wrong tool for an unplanned outage."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Detach the us-west-2 secondary cluster from the global database and promote it to a standalone writable cluster, then report the replication lag observed at the time of failure as the realized RPO.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Detach-and-promote (or a managed failover that allows data loss) is the unplanned recovery path, and the cross-Region replication lag at the moment of failure is exactly the committed data that never reached the secondary."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Restore the most recent automated cross-Region snapshot of the primary cluster into us-west-2 as a new cluster, then report the age of that snapshot as the amount of data lost in the incident.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Restoring from a snapshot discards everything replicated since the snapshot was taken and can take well beyond 15 minutes, producing far worse RPO and RTO than promoting the up-to-date secondary."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Detach the us-west-2 secondary cluster from the global database and promote it to a standalone writable cluster, then report zero data loss because Aurora replicates storage blocks synchronously between Regions.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Promotion is correct, but Aurora Global Database uses asynchronous storage-level replication with typical sub-second lag, so claiming zero loss misstates the RPO to the business."
      }
    ],
