    "service": "Route 53",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A ticketing platform runs in two Regions with Route 53 failover between them. In one incident, the /health endpoint returned 200 while the Region's database was down, so no failover occurred and users saw errors for an hour. The team then rewrote the endpoint to test every dependency, including a third-party payment gateway; the next time that gateway had a brief global slowdown, both Regions' checks failed simultaneously and Route 53 flapped traffic between two Regions that were each serving most requests successfully. How should the health check design be corrected?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Keep the full-dependency endpoint in both Regions but shorten the health check interval and lower the failure threshold so that Route 53 detects and reacts to real outages faster.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Faster detection makes the flapping worse: any shared-dependency slowdown still fails both Regions at once, and lower thresholds convert brief blips into more frequent false failovers."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Replace the endpoint with a simple TCP health check against each Region's load balancer listener so that transient application-level errors can no longer trigger an unnecessary regional failover.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "A TCP listener check is the original shallow-check problem in another form; it would again report healthy while the Region's database or application logic is failing."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Scope each Region's endpoint to verify only its own critical dependencies, and combine it with alarm-based and calculated health checks so shared-dependency failures cannot fail both Regions.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Checking only Region-local dependencies makes the signal mean 'this Region is worse than the other,' and composing it with alarm-based and calculated checks adds thresholds that suppress cascading false failovers."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Delete the custom health checks and enable Evaluate Target Health on the alias records so that Route 53 relies on the load balancer's own view of its registered targets in each Region.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Evaluate Target Health reflects only whether load balancer targets pass their checks, so a healthy fleet in front of a failed database would again mask the dependency outage."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "c"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "c"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "c"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Health checks should test Region-local critical dependencies only and be composed with calculated and alarm-based checks so shared failures cannot take down both Regions' signals.",
    "explanation": "A failover health check must answer one question: is this Region less able to serve traffic than the other one. Including dependencies shared by both Regions, such as a third-party gateway, guarantees correlated false failures, while checking nothing but the listener misses real regional outages. The robust pattern scopes the deep check to dependencies unique to the Region and layers Route 53 calculated and CloudWatch alarm-based health checks on top to add thresholds and hysteresis against flapping.",
