    "service": "Amazon EKS",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A media company runs a stream-processing application with three replicas on an Amazon EKS cluster that uses a managed node group spanning three Availability Zones. During the last Kubernetes version upgrade, the rolling node replacement drained pods faster than replacement nodes became ready in every zone, and at one point all three replicas were rescheduled onto nodes in a single Availability Zone. A zone impairment during a future upgrade would therefore take down the entire application. The company wants replicas to remain distributed across zones throughout node-group upgrades. What should a solutions architect do to meet these requirements?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Configure a pod disruption budget with maxUnavailable set to 1 so that the upgrade process evicts only one replica of the application at a time from the node group.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "A pod disruption budget controls how quickly pods are evicted, but it places no constraint on where evicted replicas are rescheduled, so they can still all land in one zone."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Add preferred pod anti-affinity on the kubernetes.io/hostname label so that the scheduler avoids placing two replicas of the application on the same worker node.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Hostname anti-affinity separates replicas across nodes, not zones, and the preferred variant is best-effort, so under upgrade pressure the scheduler may still colocate replicas in a single zone."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Split the workload into three separate deployments and use a nodeSelector on each one to pin its replica to nodes in a specific Availability Zone of the cluster.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Zone-pinned replicas cannot be rescheduled anywhere while their zone's nodes are being replaced, so the upgrade itself would take replicas offline, and three deployments add ongoing management burden."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Define a topology spread constraint on the topology.kubernetes.io/zone label with maxSkew set to 1 and whenUnsatisfiable set to DoNotSchedule for the deployment.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "A zone-based topology spread constraint with DoNotSchedule makes the scheduler enforce, rather than merely prefer, an even zone distribution, so replicas rescheduled during the upgrade can never all land in one zone."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "d"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "d"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "d"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Enforce a zone topology spread constraint with maxSkew 1 and DoNotSchedule.",
    "explanation": "Pod topology spread constraints on topology.kubernetes.io/zone with whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule are the mechanism that hard-enforces zone distribution while pods churn during node-group upgrades. Disruption budgets pace evictions without controlling placement, hostname anti-affinity works at node rather than zone granularity, and zone pinning prevents rescheduling exactly when nodes are being replaced.",
