    "service": "Amazon DynamoDB",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A fintech company runs a fleet of six EC2 instances that each execute the same in-house cron framework for nightly settlement jobs; the framework must be retained because it integrates tightly with proprietary batch tooling. A leader-election bug occasionally lets two instances believe they are the active scheduler at the same time, and duplicated settlement runs have double-posted transactions to customer accounts. The company wants a guarantee that at most one instance runs a given job even when the election misbehaves. Which solution will meet these requirements?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Store a lease item in a DynamoDB table that each instance acquires with a conditional write that succeeds only when the lease is unheld or expired, and run jobs only while holding it.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "DynamoDB conditional writes are atomic, so exactly one instance's acquisition succeeds even when several attempt it simultaneously, and the expiry lets a surviving instance take over if the holder dies."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Wrap each settlement job's database updates inside a DynamoDB transaction so that two duplicate runs of the same job cannot both commit conflicting changes to the same settlement records.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Transactions make one run's writes atomic but do not stop a second scheduler from executing the job, so side effects outside the table, such as postings to external ledgers, still occur twice."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Publish every job trigger to an SQS FIFO queue with content-based deduplication enabled so that duplicate triggers emitted by competing schedulers are silently dropped before execution.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "FIFO deduplication only suppresses identical messages within a five-minute window, so duplicate triggers sent later, or with differing payload details, are both delivered and both executed."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Replace the instance-based schedulers with Amazon EventBridge Scheduler so that every settlement job is invoked from a single fully managed schedule with no election at all.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "A single managed scheduler would remove the election, but it requires abandoning the in-house cron framework that the company has stated must be retained for its proprietary batch integration."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "a"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "a"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "a"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Guard each job with a DynamoDB conditional-write lease so only the acquiring instance may run it.",
    "explanation": "An atomic conditional write on a shared lease item is the standard exactly-one-runner guard: when two would-be leaders race, DynamoDB accepts only one acquisition, and a lease expiry provides liveness if the holder crashes mid-job. Transactions and FIFO deduplication narrow but do not close the double-execution window, and moving to EventBridge Scheduler violates the constraint to keep the existing framework.",
    "trigger": "DynamoDB conditional-write lease preventing duplicate cron leaders from double-running jobs",
