    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "An online marketplace coordinates orders across three microservices: a payment service that charges cards through a third-party processor, an inventory service backed by DynamoDB, and a shipping service. In a recent incident, payment charges succeeded but the subsequent inventory reservations failed because an item had sold out, leaving customers charged for orders that were never created; support staff had to find and refund the charges manually. The company wants failed orders unwound automatically so that no customer is ever left charged without a confirmed order. Which solution will meet these requirements?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Group the payment, inventory, and shipping updates into a single DynamoDB transaction so that all three changes either commit together in full or are rolled back automatically as one atomic unit.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "DynamoDB transactions span only DynamoDB items; the card charge executes at an external third-party processor and cannot participate in or be rolled back by the transaction."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Orchestrate the order as a Step Functions workflow in which each service call defines a compensating action, and use Catch transitions to run compensations in reverse when a later step fails.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "This is the saga pattern: when the inventory reservation fails, the workflow's Catch path automatically invokes the payment refund compensation, so no customer stays charged without an order."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Implement a two-phase commit protocol coordinated by the order service so that every participating service prepares and confirms each order before any card charge is finalized.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Two-phase commit requires every participant to support prepare and hold locks, which the third-party card processor does not offer, and it couples the availability of all three services together."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Send failed inventory reservations to a dead-letter queue and configure a Lambda function that retries the failed reservations with exponential backoff until stock becomes available again.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "A sold-out item is a business failure that retries cannot fix; the reservation may never succeed, and while retries continue the customer remains charged for an order that does not exist."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "b"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "b"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "b"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Run the order as a Step Functions saga whose Catch paths invoke compensating actions in reverse.",
    "explanation": "Because the charge happens at an external processor, no distributed ACID transaction can cover all steps; the saga pattern instead pairs every forward action with a compensating action, and Step Functions Catch transitions run those compensations in reverse order when a later step fails. That automatically refunds the payment when inventory reservation fails, while transactions, two-phase commit, and blind retries each fail on the external dependency or the permanent sell-out.",
