    "id": "EX-P3-02",
    "objectiveId": "EX-P3-02",
    "objectiveName": "Amazon Aurora: Fix read-your-writes UX broken by replica lag without abandoning read scaling.",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "High-Performing Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "High-Performing Architectures",
    "service": "Amazon Aurora",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A SaaS company runs an Amazon Aurora MySQL cluster with one writer and two Aurora Replicas. The application sends all writes to the cluster endpoint and all reads to the reader endpoint. After users save profile changes, they are redirected to their profile page, and roughly 2% of the time the page shows the old values because replica lag averages 150 ms. The company must fix this experience while continuing to offload the bulk of read traffic from the writer. What should a solutions architect recommend?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Modify the application so the read issued immediately after a user's own write goes to the cluster writer endpoint, while all other read queries continue to use the reader endpoint.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Routing only the post-write read to the writer guarantees read-your-writes consistency for that request while preserving replica offload for the vast majority of read traffic."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Increase the number of Aurora Replicas from two to four so the reader endpoint spreads queries across more instances and each replica applies changes with lower lag.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Adding replicas distributes read volume but does nothing to shrink asynchronous replication lag on any individual replica, so stale post-write reads would continue to occur."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Deploy RDS Proxy in front of the cluster and point all application read traffic at the proxy's read-only endpoint so that queries are load balanced across the replicas at the statement level.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "A proxy read-only endpoint still serves queries from asynchronously replicated replicas, so the read that follows a write can still return stale data during the lag window."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Upgrade both Aurora Replica instances to a larger instance class so replication apply keeps pace with the writer and lag is eliminated for reads served by the replicas.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Larger replicas may reduce average lag, but Aurora replication remains asynchronous, so lag can never be guaranteed to be zero and the stale-read window is only narrowed, not closed."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "a"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "a"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "a"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Send the immediate post-write read to the writer endpoint; keep other reads on the reader endpoint.",
    "explanation": "The two constraints are read-your-writes consistency for the redirect and continued read offloading. Because Aurora Replica reads are eventually consistent, the only deterministic fix is to serve the session's post-write read from the writer. Adding, enlarging, or proxying replicas shrinks or redistributes lag but cannot eliminate the consistency gap.",
