    "service": "Amazon ElastiCache",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A retail platform stores its 2-million-item product catalog in Amazon RDS for MySQL. The read-to-write ratio is 50:1, merchandising staff update a few hundred products several times per day, and the business confirms that shoppers may see prices that are up to 10 minutes old. The team is adding an ElastiCache for Redis layer and wants to reduce database read load while keeping the caching logic as simple as possible. Which caching approach should a solutions architect recommend?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Use a write-through strategy in which every catalog update writes to Redis and the database together, keeping every cached item permanently synchronized with the source database.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Write-through keeps the cache fresh but adds latency and code to the write path and fills the cache with items nobody is browsing, complexity the 10-minute staleness allowance makes unnecessary."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Use a lazy loading strategy with a 10-minute TTL so only items that shoppers actually request are cached and any stale entry expires within the tolerated staleness window.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Lazy loading populates the cache only with demanded items, and the TTL bounds staleness to exactly the window the business accepts, achieving the goal with minimal application logic."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Preload the full catalog into Redis with a nightly batch job and serve all reads from the cache without expiration until the following night's reload replaces the data.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "A nightly reload means intraday price updates could be invisible for up to 24 hours, which violates the stated 10-minute staleness tolerance for shoppers."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Use a lazy loading strategy without any TTL and have the application flush the entire cache whenever merchandising staff change any product record in the database.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Flushing the whole cache on every update discards millions of valid entries and triggers a thundering herd of database reads, far more disruption than expiring individual entries by TTL."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "b"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "b"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "b"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Lazy loading with a 10-minute TTL matches the staleness tolerance with the simplest logic.",
