    "service": "Amazon Aurora",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A Java microservice reads from an Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL cluster with three Aurora Replicas through the cluster reader endpoint. The service opens a fixed pool of 60 database connections at startup and keeps them open indefinitely. CloudWatch shows one replica at 90% CPU while the other two sit below 10%, and the imbalance persists for days. The team wants read traffic spread across all three replicas without moving load onto the writer. What should a solutions architect recommend?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Increase the number of Aurora Replicas from three to five so the reader endpoint has more instances available when it distributes the incoming queries.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "The reader endpoint balances at connection time, not per query, so the 60 already-established connections stay pinned to the same replica no matter how many new replicas exist."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Disable DNS caching in the JVM and lower the client resolver TTL so each query resolves the reader endpoint again and reaches a different replica instance.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "DNS resolution only happens when a new connection is established; existing pooled connections never re-resolve, so tuning resolver caching alone does not rebalance a static pool."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Configure a maximum connection lifetime in the pool so connections are periodically recycled, letting the reader endpoint's DNS rotation spread them across replicas.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Recycling connections on a bounded lifetime forces regular reconnects, and each reconnect passes through the endpoint's round-robin DNS answer, redistributing the pool across all replicas over time."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Point the connection pool at the cluster endpoint instead of the reader endpoint so that queries are shared across the writer instance and the three replica instances.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "The cluster endpoint always targets the current writer, so this shifts the entire read pool onto the writer, which violates the requirement to keep read load off that instance."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "c"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "c"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "c"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Set a max connection lifetime so pooled connections recycle and redistribute across readers.",
    "explanation": "The reader endpoint is round-robin DNS evaluated only at connection establishment, so a long-lived static pool created in one burst can pin to a single replica. The fix must force periodic reconnection: a bounded connection lifetime lets the endpoint redistribute connections naturally. More replicas or DNS tweaks do not touch existing sockets, and the cluster endpoint routes to the writer.",
