    "id": "EX-P3-12",
    "objectiveId": "EX-P3-12",
    "objectiveName": "Amazon DynamoDB: Restructure to single-item atomicity instead of paying the 2x TransactWriteItems premium.",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "High-Performing Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "High-Performing Architectures",
    "service": "Amazon DynamoDB",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A subscription billing service stores each customer's plan record and usage counters as two separate items that always share the same partition key in an Amazon DynamoDB table. Every billing update calls TransactWriteItems to change both items atomically. The two items are never written independently. As traffic grew, the team noticed write capacity costs running about twice the modeled amount and p99 write latency well above single-item writes. Which change reduces both cost and latency while preserving atomic updates?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Replace the TransactWriteItems calls with BatchWriteItem requests that submit both item updates together so the writes are grouped into one lower-cost API operation.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "BatchWriteItem is not atomic; individual puts can fail and be retried independently, so the plan record and counters could diverge, breaking the atomicity requirement."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Place a DynamoDB Accelerator cluster in front of the table so the transactional writes complete against the cache first and replicate to the table asynchronously.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "DAX is a write-through cache that forwards writes synchronously to DynamoDB rather than absorbing them asynchronously, so transactional write cost and latency are not reduced."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Switch the table from provisioned capacity to on-demand mode so the transactional writes are billed per request instead of consuming pre-purchased write capacity units.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "On-demand changes how capacity is billed, not how much a transaction consumes; transactional writes still cost double per item in either mode, and latency is unchanged."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Merge the plan record and usage counters into a single item and perform each billing change with one UpdateItem call that uses a condition expression where needed.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "A single-item UpdateItem is inherently atomic, consumes standard rather than doubled write capacity, and completes in one round trip, cutting both cost and latency."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "d"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "d"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "d"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Merge the always-together items into one and use atomic single-item UpdateItem calls.",
    "explanation": "TransactWriteItems consumes twice the write capacity of standard writes and performs a two-phase commit, which explains both the doubled bill and the elevated p99. Since the two items always change together and share a partition key, they are one logical entity; combining them makes every update a naturally atomic single-item write. Batching sacrifices atomicity, and DAX or billing-mode changes leave transaction overhead intact.",
    "trigger": "Restructure to single-item atomicity instead of paying the 2x TransactWriteItems premium.",
