    "id": "EX-P3-15",
    "objectiveId": "EX-P3-15",
    "objectiveName": "Amazon Redshift: Fix a skewed, spilling Redshift join with matching KEY distribution and sort keys.",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "High-Performing Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "High-Performing Architectures",
    "service": "Amazon Redshift",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "multiple",
    "question": "A retail analytics team runs a nightly Amazon Redshift job that joins an 8-billion-row sales fact table to a 2-billion-row order_details table on order_id, with a 500-row date dimension joined afterward. Both large tables use EVEN distribution and have no sort keys. The query plan shows DS_DIST_BOTH broadcasts, and system tables confirm intermediate results spilling to disk, pushing the job past its window. Which combination of changes should a solutions architect make to speed up the join? (Choose two.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Change both of the large tables to ALL distribution so every compute node holds a complete copy and the join requires no data redistribution at run time.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "ALL distribution is intended for small dimension tables; replicating multibillion-row tables to every node would multiply storage consumption and make loads and vacuums far slower."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Resize the cluster by adding more compute nodes so that the redistributed join data is spread across more slices and each node spills less intermediate data to local disk.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "More nodes add memory but every query still pays to shuffle billions of rows across the network, so the structural DS_DIST_BOTH redistribution cost remains in the plan."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Set order_id as the distribution key on both the sales table and the order_details table so rows with matching join values are stored on the same slices.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "A shared distribution key collocates matching rows, converting DS_DIST_BOTH into a local DS_DIST_NONE join and eliminating the cross-node shuffle that drives the spill."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Change the 500-row date dimension from ALL distribution to EVEN distribution so its storage footprint is balanced evenly across all of the compute nodes.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Tiny dimensions are the textbook case for ALL distribution; spreading 500 rows with EVEN would force redistribution or broadcasts during the join for no meaningful storage benefit."
      },
      {
        "id": "e",
        "text": "Define order_id as the sort key on both large tables so the planner can choose a merge join over the collocated data and cut the intermediate hash memory footprint.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "With both tables distributed and sorted on the join column, Redshift can use a merge join that streams sorted rows, avoiding large hash tables and the associated disk spill."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "c",
      "e"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "c",
      "e"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "c",
      "e"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Distribute both large tables on order_id and sort them on order_id to collocate and merge-join.",
    "explanation": "DS_DIST_BOTH with disk spill signals that neither large table is distributed on the join column, so Redshift redistributes both at run time and hashes billions of rows. Making order_id the distribution key on both tables collocates the join, and adding it as the sort key enables an efficient merge join with minimal memory. ALL on huge tables, cluster resizing, and un-ALLing the tiny dimension attack the wrong lever.",
