    "objectiveName": "AWS PrivateLink: Scaling interface VPC endpoint throughput by adding endpoint ENIs across AZs",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "High-Performing Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "High-Performing Architectures",
    "service": "AWS PrivateLink",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A hedge fund consumes a market-data provider's API over AWS PrivateLink. The consuming VPC spans three Availability Zones, but the interface endpoint was created with a single subnet, so all traffic funnels through one endpoint network interface in one AZ. During market open, the fund's ingest fleet drives sustained multi-gigabit throughput and connections begin to stall while the provider's Network Load Balancer shows spare capacity. The fund needs more throughput to the service without changes on the provider side. What should a solutions architect do?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Migrate the ingest fleet to larger instance sizes so each consuming instance has a higher available network bandwidth allowance.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "The bottleneck is the aggregate capacity of the single endpoint network interface, not the instances' own bandwidth allowances, so larger instances still contend for the same ENI."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Add subnets in the two remaining Availability Zones to the interface endpoint so traffic is distributed across additional endpoint network interfaces.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Each AZ added to an interface endpoint provisions another endpoint ENI with its own bandwidth capacity, and zone-local resolution spreads consumer traffic across the ENIs."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Replace the interface endpoint with a gateway endpoint so the traffic uses route-table entries instead of passing through network interfaces.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Gateway endpoints exist only for Amazon S3 and DynamoDB; a partner's endpoint service published through PrivateLink can only be consumed through interface endpoints."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Ask the provider to enable cross-zone load balancing on the Network Load Balancer behind the endpoint service so requests spread over more of the provider's targets.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Cross-zone load balancing changes distribution among the provider's targets, which already have spare capacity; it does nothing for the consumer-side ENI bandwidth limit."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "b"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "b"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "b"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Expand the interface endpoint into the other two AZs so each additional endpoint ENI contributes its own bandwidth to the aggregate.",
    "explanation": "Interface endpoint throughput scales per endpoint network interface, and an endpoint gets one ENI per subnet you associate with it. Adding the remaining AZs multiplies the available bandwidth and lets instances in each zone use a local ENI, which also removes cross-AZ hops. Instance sizing and provider-side load balancing settings do not relieve a consumer-side per-ENI bottleneck, and gateway endpoints are not available for partner services.",
