    "question": "A retailer's Java order service calls an internal Application Load Balancer in front of a pricing tier. During flash sales the ALB scales out and its node IP addresses change, but a large share of order-service traffic keeps hammering the original addresses: some nodes are overloaded while new ones sit idle, and connection errors appear when old nodes are retired. The operations team verifies the ALB's DNS record has a 60-second TTL and that freshly started order-service instances balance correctly until the next scaling event. What should a solutions architect do?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Raise the TTL on the load balancer's DNS record so the answers that clients receive remain valid across future scaling events.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "A longer TTL would make clients hold stale addresses even longer; the short TTL exists precisely so clients track the load balancer's changing node set."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Set the JVM's networkaddress.cache.ttl property to a low value so the service re-resolves the load balancer name and honors its short DNS TTL.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "By default the JVM can cache successful DNS lookups indefinitely, so a low cache TTL makes the client re-resolve and discover new and retired load balancer nodes promptly."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Enable HTTP keep-alive in the order service so its established connections migrate automatically to the newly added load balancer nodes.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Keep-alive pins traffic to the nodes a connection already reaches; it cannot move existing connections to nodes the client has never resolved."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Point the order service at the load balancer's zonal DNS names so that each client resolves only the load balancer node in its own Availability Zone.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Zonal records still return addresses that change as the load balancer scales, so a client that caches lookups forever has the same staleness problem."
      }
    ],
