    "service": "Amazon CloudFront",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "multiple",
    "question": "A media publisher serves article images and static JavaScript from an Amazon S3 origin through a CloudFront distribution. The application appends utm_campaign, utm_source, and a per-user sessionid query string parameter to every asset URL, and the distribution's cache policy currently includes all query strings and all cookies in the cache key. Only a lang query string parameter actually changes the bytes that are returned, and the assets are identical for every signed-in user. CloudFront reporting shows a 40% cache hit ratio, and the origin is handling five times more requests than forecast. Which combination of changes will MOST improve the cache hit ratio? (Choose two.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Attach an origin request policy that forwards all query strings and headers to the origin so that the origin can normalize the responses itself.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "An origin request policy controls only what is forwarded to the origin on a miss; it does not change the cache key, so identical assets are still cached under many distinct keys."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Update the cache policy so that the cache key includes only the lang query string parameter and ignores all other query string parameters.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Whitelisting only the parameter that changes the response collapses the utm_* and sessionid variations into a single cached copy per language, directly raising the hit ratio."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Enable Origin Shield in the AWS Region nearest the S3 bucket to add a consolidated caching layer between the edge locations and the origin.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Origin Shield reduces duplicate requests that reach the origin, but it does not fix the fragmented cache key, so the viewer-facing hit ratio at the edge stays low."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Increase the default TTL and maximum TTL on the cache behavior to 30 days so that cached copies of the assets persist longer at the edge.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Longer TTLs cannot help when every user generates a unique cache key; each sessionid variant is still a first-time miss no matter how long it could be retained."
      },
      {
        "id": "e",
        "text": "Update the cache policy so that the session cookie and other cookies are excluded from the cache key for the distribution's static asset paths.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Static assets are identical for all users, so removing per-user cookies from the cache key lets one cached object serve every viewer instead of one copy per session."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "b",
      "e"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "b",
      "e"
    ],
