    "question": "An online retailer deploys its React storefront up to 20 times per day. Each build produces new CSS and JavaScript bundles, which are uploaded to Amazon S3 and served through CloudFront under an /assets/ path. After each deployment the release pipeline submits invalidation requests for several thousand individual asset paths, which take minutes to propagate and now exceed the 1,000 free invalidation paths each month. The retailer wants users to receive updated assets immediately after every deployment. Which solution meets these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Embed a content hash in each bundle file name during the build, reference the new file names from the HTML, and cache the bundles with a long TTL.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "A changed file gets a new URL, so browsers and edge locations fetch it on the first request with no invalidation at all, while unchanged bundles stay cached; updates are immediate and free."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Replace the per-file invalidation requests with a single wildcard invalidation for /assets/* after each deployment to stay inside the free tier.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "A wildcard counts as one path, but invalidations still take time to propagate through edge locations, so updates are not immediate, and every cached bundle is discarded on each deployment."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Set the maximum TTL of the /assets/ cache behavior to 0 seconds so that every request is revalidated against the S3 origin before it is served.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Forcing revalidation on every request delivers fresh assets but removes the benefit of edge caching, increasing latency and S3 request costs on every single page view."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Configure the origin to return a Cache-Control: no-store header on all bundles so that CloudFront always retrieves the newest copy from Amazon S3.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "The no-store directive prevents CloudFront from caching the bundles entirely, which pushes all asset traffic to the origin and raises both latency and data transfer costs."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "a"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "a"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "a"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Use content-hashed (versioned) bundle file names with long TTLs instead of invalidation requests.",
    "explanation": "Content-hashed file names make every release self-invalidating: new bundles have new URLs that miss the cache exactly once, and old bundles simply age out. This delivers instant updates with zero invalidation cost and full edge caching, unlike wildcard invalidations (propagation delay, cache churn) or disabling caching (latency and origin cost).",
    "trigger": "Choose versioned/hashed asset names over invalidation requests for instant, low-cost asset updates.",
