    "id": "EX-P5-05",
    "objectiveId": "EX-P5-05",
    "objectiveName": "Amazon CloudFront: Edge compression needs both the behavior's compress setting and Gzip/Brotli enabled in the cache policy.",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "High-Performing Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "High-Performing Architectures",
    "service": "Amazon CloudFront",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "A travel booking site serves HTML and JSON responses of 100 KB to 600 KB through a CloudFront distribution with an Application Load Balancer origin. To cut page load times on slow connections, an engineer selected the Compress objects automatically setting on the cache behavior and attached a custom cache policy the team had created earlier. Browser testing shows that viewers send Accept-Encoding: gzip, br, and the origin returns the content without any Content-Encoding header, yet responses still arrive uncompressed. Which action will MOST likely resolve the issue?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Attach an origin request policy that forwards the viewer's Accept-Encoding header unchanged so the Application Load Balancer can negotiate compression.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Forwarding Accept-Encoding moves the negotiation to the origin, but the application does not compress responses, and it would bypass the edge compression the team wants."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Update the custom cache policy to enable Gzip and Brotli support so that CloudFront normalizes the Accept-Encoding header and compresses at the edge.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Edge compression requires the cache policy to have Gzip and Brotli support enabled; without those settings CloudFront does not cache compressed variants, so eligible objects are served uncompressed."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Add a CloudFront Function on the viewer response event that sets the Content-Encoding header to br for all of the compressible content types.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Setting a Content-Encoding header does not compress the body; clients would try to decode plain bytes as Brotli and fail, so this breaks responses rather than shrinking them."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Lower the minimum TTL of the cache behavior to 0 seconds so that CloudFront revalidates each object and retrieves a compressed copy from the origin.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "TTL settings control freshness, not encoding; revalidating more often still returns uncompressed bodies because neither the origin nor the edge is producing compressed variants."
      }
    ],
