    "id": "EX-P5-06",
    "objectiveId": "EX-P5-06",
    "objectiveName": "Lambda@Edge: Pick Lambda@Edge origin-request over CloudFront Functions when routing needs an external data lookup.",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "High-Performing Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "High-Performing Architectures",
    "service": "Lambda@Edge",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "single",
    "question": "An ad-tech company is running an experiment that must route 10% of requests for its landing pages to a redesigned origin. Each viewer's experiment assignment is stored in an Amazon DynamoDB table keyed on a device identifier that arrives in a request header, and assignments change during the test, so they cannot be hard-coded into edge logic. The company wants the routing decision made within CloudFront so that both origins stay behind one distribution and one domain name. Which solution will meet these requirements?",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Deploy a CloudFront Function on the viewer request event that queries the DynamoDB table for the assignment and rewrites the request to the new origin.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "CloudFront Functions run in a restricted sub-millisecond environment with no network access, so they cannot call DynamoDB or any other external service."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Deploy a Lambda@Edge function on the viewer response event that reads the assignment from the DynamoDB table and returns a redirect to the new origin.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "The viewer response event fires after the origin has already answered, so redirecting from there adds a full extra round trip and still cannot change which origin served the request."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Deploy a Lambda@Edge function on the origin request event that looks up the assignment in the DynamoDB table and changes the request's origin accordingly.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Lambda@Edge can make network calls, and the origin request event fires before CloudFront contacts the origin, which is exactly where the origin can be swapped per request."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Deploy a CloudFront Function on the origin request event that reads the assignment from the CloudFront key-value store and switches the request's origin accordingly.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "CloudFront Functions support only the viewer request and viewer response events, and the key-value store would still need an external process to mirror every changing DynamoDB assignment."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "c"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "c"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "c"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Lambda@Edge on the origin request event: it can query DynamoDB and swap the origin per request.",
    "explanation": "Only Lambda@Edge has the network access needed for the DynamoDB lookup, and only the origin request event lets edge code change the origin before CloudFront forwards the request. CloudFront Functions are limited to viewer events with no network calls, and viewer response logic runs too late to influence origin selection.",
