    "id": "EX-P6-13",
    "objectiveId": "EX-P6-13",
    "objectiveName": "AWS Glue: Shrink a full-rescan Glue batch via bookmarks, bounded execution, and parallel partition pushdown.",
    "variant": 1,
    "domain": "High-Performing Architectures",
    "sourceDomain": "High-Performing Architectures",
    "service": "AWS Glue",
    "difficulty": "Hard",
    "type": "multiple",
    "question": "A marketing analytics firm runs a nightly AWS Glue Spark job that reads a date-partitioned S3 dataset covering three years, even though only the newest day of data changes between runs. The job currently takes six hours, and an upcoming backfill will add 14 months of historical files that must be worked through gradually without producing a single enormous run. The firm wants the nightly job to process only new data, keep individual runs bounded in size, and finish the current-day work in well under an hour. Which combination of steps should a solutions architect take? (Choose three.)",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "a",
        "text": "Convert the job to a Python shell job and raise its DPU allocation so the nightly run gets more processing capacity per invocation.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Python shell jobs run on a single node without Spark's distributed execution, so they cannot parallelize partition processing at this scale. Raising capacity on a non-distributed job type does not shorten the runtime."
      },
      {
        "id": "b",
        "text": "Enable job bookmarks on the job so each run tracks previously processed files and reads only data that has arrived since the last successful run.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Job bookmarks persist state about processed files between runs, converting the job from a full three-year rescan into an incremental pass over only new data, which is the largest single reduction in work."
      },
      {
        "id": "c",
        "text": "Set bounded execution properties on the source so each run processes at most a fixed number of unprocessed files as the backfill drains across runs.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Bounded execution caps how much of the unprocessed backlog a single run consumes, so the 14-month backfill is absorbed in controlled increments instead of one enormous run."
      },
      {
        "id": "d",
        "text": "Switch the job to the Flex execution class so runs are scheduled onto spare capacity at a lower cost per DPU-hour.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "Flex trades start-up latency and steady capacity for lower price, which can lengthen rather than shorten runs. It contributes nothing to incremental processing or bounding run size."
      },
      {
        "id": "e",
        "text": "Increase the job timeout from six hours to twelve hours so that the large backfill runs have sufficient headroom to complete without being terminated.",
        "correct": false,
        "explanation": "A longer timeout merely permits bigger runs, which is the opposite of the requirement to keep each run bounded. It does not reduce the data read or the runtime."
      },
      {
        "id": "f",
        "text": "Apply a pushdown predicate on the date partition column and provision enough workers for the selected partitions to be processed in parallel.",
        "correct": true,
        "explanation": "Partition pushdown prevents the job from listing and reading the untouched historical partitions, and adequate worker parallelism across the remaining partitions brings the nightly run well under the one-hour target."
      }
    ],
    "answers": [
      "b",
      "c",
      "f"
    ],
    "correctOptionIds": [
      "b",
      "c",
      "f"
    ],
    "sourceCorrectOptionIds": [
      "b",
      "c",
      "f"
    ],
    "answerSummary": "Enable job bookmarks, set bounded execution limits, and use partition pushdown with parallel workers.",
    "explanation": "Three mechanisms combine to meet the three stated goals: job bookmarks make processing incremental, bounded execution keeps the backfill from creating one giant run, and partition pushdown with sufficient workers keeps the nightly increment fast. Flex execution, longer timeouts, and Python shell jobs address none of the incremental-processing requirements.",
